1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Not the same as https://www.geni.com/people/John-Henry-Back/6000000013092342653 (son of Johannes Bach & Maria Herling)
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===Johann Heinrich Bach
2 CONT * AKA John Henry Back
2 CONT *BIRTH: 4 Nov 1709, Hamburg, Germany
2 CONT *DEATH: 1789 (aged 79–80) Culpeper County, Virginia, USA
2 CONT * BURIAL: Burial Details Unknown, Specifically: Buried on his farm in Culpeper County, Virginia
2 CONT * MEMORIAL ID 132887117 · View Source
2 CONT * Son of Johann Christoph Bach (1676–1738) & Anna Hoffman (1687–1739)
2 CONT * Brother of Johann Wilhelm Bach (1722–1794)
2 CONT * Husband of Anna Maria Hoffman (1707–1782)  (m. 1737)
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna Maria Hoffman was born in Rodach, Germany. Her father was John Hoffman.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna, her husband, and their 2-year-old son John sailed to America in 1740. They settled on some land along Crooked Creek, near where it joins the Robinson River. She and her husband lived there for the rest of their lives, and th
2 CONC ey were buried there as well.
2 CONT
2 CONT The four children of Anna Maria Hoffman and John Henry Back were:
2 CONT # John Back (1738–1794)
2 CONT # Henry Back (1743–1809)
2 CONT # Joseph Back (1745–1819)
2 CONT # Anna Back Strother (1755–1804)
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/132887117/johann-heinrich-bach
2 CONT
2 CONT **After he arrived in America, he changed the spelling of his name to John Henry Back.
2 CONT
2 CONT This Back/Bach family is not related to Harman Back (aka Hermann Bach), from Freudenberg, Germany. A significant amount of documented research proves this.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1994, an incorrect genealogy of this family was published, which claimed that this family descended from Harman Back from Freudenberg, even though there was not one piece of evidence to prove it. Furthermore, everyone in the fa
2 CONC mily already knew the actual genealogy of their family, which is what is shown here. Since then, several Board-Certified genealogists and other prominent researchers and historians have confirmed the actual genealogy of this famil
2 CONC y, which is what is shown here, by studying numerous primary source documents and other significant records and evidence.
2 CONT
2 CONT His family and his ancestors were from Thuringia, Germany. Please see the attached Family Tree, which was prepared by Johann Sebastian Bach in 1735. It shows the men in the family who knew how to play a musical instrument. Because
2 CONC  Johann Heinrich was included, he must have known how to play an instrument, probably an organ.
2 CONT
2 CONT Johann Heinrich's father liked to move around, and so Johann was actually born while his parents lived in Hamburg, Germany.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was also related to Johann Sebastian Bach. He was his second cousin, once removed. Johann Sebastian Bach's great grandfather was Johann Hans Bach, who was the great, great grandfather of Johann Heinrich Bach. That makes them se
2 CONC cond cousins, once removed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Johann married Anna Maria Hoffman, in Thuringia, in 1737. They had a son named John, who was born in 1738. They all sailed to America in 1740, on the ship "Lydia," which arrived in Philadelphia, on September 27, 1740.
2 CONT
2 CONT Also sailing with them was Johann's younger brother, Johann Wilhelm Bach, and their third cousin, Johann Jacob Bach (FAG #169309936). All three men took their oaths, when they arrived in Philadelphia, and their names were recorded
2 CONC  there as doing so.
2 CONT
2 CONT They settled in what later became Culpeper County, along the north side of Crooked Creek (also known as Meander Run), about one mile from where it empties into the Robinson River. It was about five miles south of the present-day t
2 CONC own of Culpeper.
2 CONT
2 CONT Johann eventually owned 786 acres there, and he worked as a millwright. He built and sold gristmills throughout the area. He also operated several of them. (In the early 1900s, Dr. Wilgus Bach wrote in his manuscript that Johann "
2 CONC ...became a successful farmer and millwright, buying the sites, erecting the mills, and selling them at fabulous prices.")
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1762, Johann bought an old family Bible from his cousin back in Europe, Johann Christian Bach. (He was a son of Johann Sebastian Bach.) When it arrived, Johann wrote inside of it, "We Came from Thuringia." (The old book was act
2 CONC ually a Catechism, which is similar to a Bible.) NOTE: Dr. B.C. Holtsclaw of The Germanna Foundation, and Dr. Wilgus Bach, both wrote about this handwritten statement being inside of this old book. But it was cut out with a razor 
2 CONC blade, around 1969. Attached are excerpts from their two books, where they each stated that they had seen that statement in the old book.
2 CONT
2 CONT One of Johann's neighbors was John Hoffman and his wife Maria Sabina Folg. (They lived along the Robison River, near where Crooked Creek flowed into it.) Two of Johann's sons, John and Henry, married two of John Hoffman's daughter
2 CONC s, Margaret and Elizabeth. John and Henry each lived on the 150 acres of land that each girl had inherited from her father.
2 CONT
2 CONT Johann's son Joseph married Elizabeth Hoffman-Maggard, and they later migrated to southeastern Kentucky. Joseph had inherited the old Bach Family Bible, and he took it with him to Kentucky.
2 CONT
2 CONT Johann's daughter Anna married Benjamin Strother and they later migrated to Fauquier County, Virginia. On their wedding day, Benjamin wrote a poem to his bride, in the old Bach Famil Bible and also signed his name.
2 CONT
2 CONT Johann Heinrich Bach was buried near his cabin, as was his wife.
2 CONT
2 CONT Please note that, after Johann Heinrich Bach arrived in America, he changed the spelling of his name to John Henry Back. Like most immigrants, he changed the spelling of his name to a more Americanized version so that people could
2 CONC  more easily pronounce it. As it was the German custom, back in Germany, when men had two first names, they used their first first name in public records, and they also signed documents with that name, but at home and in personal 
2 CONC converations, they used their second first name (or middle name). Therefore, in public records, John Henry Back was known as John Back, but in personal conversation, he was known as Henry.
2 CONT
2 CONT Johann married Anna Maria Hoffman, in Thuringia, in 1737. They had a son named John, who was born in 1738. They all sailed to America in 1740, on the ship "Lydia," which arrived in Philadelphia, on September 27, 1740.
2 CONT
2 CONT Two of Johann's sons, John and Henry, married two of John Hoffman's daughters, Margaret and Elizabeth. John and Henry each lived on the 150 acres of land that each girl had inherited from her father.
2 CONT
2 CONT Johann's son Joseph married Elizabeth Hoffman-Maggard, and they later migrated to southeastern Kentucky. Joseph had inherited the old Bach Family Bible from his father, and he took it with him to Kentucky. (That's the book that is
2 CONC  now at the Breathitt County Library.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Johann's daughter Anna married Benjamin Strother and they later migrated to Fauquier County, Virginia. On their wedding day, Benjamin wrote a poem to his bride, in the old Bach Family Bible and also signed his name. That entry can
2 CONC  still be seen today.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===References
2 CONT * https://sites.rootsweb.com/~bachgenealogy/
2 CONT *https://sites.rootsweb.com/~theshipoliver/Hermann%20kids%20vs2.jpg
2 CONT * https://sites.rootsweb.com/~theshipoliver/
2 CONT * https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/132887117/johann-heinrich-bach
2 CONT * https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/167724221/anna-maria-bach
