1 NAME Johann Wilhelm /Bach/
2 GIVN Johann Wilhelm
2 SURN Bach
2 _MARNM Back
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1722
2 ADDR
3 CITY Rotterdam
3 STAE ZH
3 CTRY Netherlands
1 DEAT
2 DATE 1794
2 ADDR
3 STAE VA
3 CTRY United States
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/167726001/johann-wilhelm-bach
2 CONT
2 CONT He was born in Rotterdam, but he grew up in Thuringia, Germany.
2 CONT
2 CONT When he was 18 years old, he sailed to America with his older brother, Johann Heinrich, and his wife and son, and his cousin, Johann Jacob Bach (FAG #169310220).
2 CONT
2 CONT Copies of the passenger lists showing the names of these three men can be found here: passenger lists
2 CONT
2 CONT The Americanized version of his name is John William Back, and so he became known as "John Back," or sometimes, "John Beck."
2 CONT
2 CONT Around 1759, he married a woman whose first name was Mary. Her middle name may have been Barbara, or her maiden name may have been Barberry or Marberry. They had at least two children: John (born June 25, 1760) and Jacob (born Mar
2 CONC ch 9, 1764). Their son John was later known as John Back, and their son Jacob was later known as Jacob Back.
2 CONT
2 CONT Johann Wilhelm and his family left Culpeper County around 1772, and they migrated to some land that was located along Copper Creek, in southwestern Virginia, right where it flows into the Clinch River. (It was then in Fincastle Co
2 CONC unty. It soon became Washington County, and then it became Russell County, in 1786.)
2 CONT
2 CONT On June 20, 1785, he bought 250 acres "on the waters of Copper Creek, a branch of the Clinch River." (Virginia Land Office Grants Q, 1785, p. 404 (Reel 57). He paid one pound, 5 shillings sterling. It had been surveyed on 5-10-178
2 CONC 2.
2 CONT
2 CONT On July 4, 1785, he bought 140 acres "on the forks of Copper Creek, a branch of the Clinch River adjoining Joshua Roman." (Virginia Land Office Grants R, 1785, P. 5 (Reel 58). He paid 15 shillings sterling. It had also been survey
2 CONC ed n 5-10-1782.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is suspected that these two parcels were adjacent to each other. He had a total of 390 acres, with these two purchases. Sometime after that, and before 1792, he bought an additional 30 acres (probably along Moccasin Creek nearb
2 CONC y). He was listed in the Land Tax Lists of Russell County as owning 420 acres. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After Johann Wilhelm died, in 1794, his widow was listed on the 1795 tax list in Russell County, VA as "Mary Marberry Back." (Marberry may have been Barberry, or perhaps, Barbara.) It is believed that she died, later in 1795, beca
2 CONC use she was no longer seen on any tax list there.
2 CONT
2 CONT After he died, in 1794, two of his great nephews (Joseph Back Jr. and his brother John Back) went to his farm and helped his widow Mary settle the estate. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Johann Wilhelm and his wife Mary were probably both buried on that farm.
2 CONT
2 CONT His great nephew John Back married Catherine Robertson in 1795, who lived a short distance away, with her family. Since his great uncle's farm was then vacant, he decided to buy it and live there. They ended up living there, until
2 CONC  1810, when they went to southeastern Kentucky.
2 CONT
2 CONT UPDATE: The Library of Virginia maintains a card file with the historical records collected by Gordon Aronhime, in the area around the Holston-Clinch River, in the 1700s. There are two cards about John Back (now attached to this m
2 CONC emorial). The cards refer to him as John Beck Sr., but his last name was first written as Back. It says that he moved to the Clinch River with his son, John Jr. (the man who served in the Rev. War). It shows that he lived on Coppe
2 CONC r Creek (later in Russell County). On Jan. 27, 1775, it looks like there was a letter from WP to AC, in which AC had engaged John to go to New Pines to see a salt peter mine (unreadable—in exchange?) with WP, for a powder mill wit
2 CONC h letter (unreadable) re powder mill. This was also in the Draper manuscripts. The card shows that his sons were John Jr., and Jacob, both of Virginia. (Both are linked here, on FAG.) On August 20, 1777, he administered the estate
2 CONC  for Nathaniel Brown with Humphrey & Henry Dickenson, and the security was 50 pounds. On May 20, 1783, he donated one hog for use by the Washington County militia (unreadable) under command of Lt. John Berry. On June 27, 1794, he 
2 CONC sold to John Harris a tract of 140 acres in the forks of Copper Creek, adjacent to Joshua Romans (Russell County). It appears that he had settled that land in 1775, and got ownership of it on May 10 1782. There appears to have bee
2 CONC n another 250 acres on Copper Creek, which he had settled in 1774, and he got ownership of it on May 6, 1782.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References
2 CONT * https://sites.rootsweb.com/~bachgenealogy/
