1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Not the same as https://www.geni.com/people/Joseph-Back/6000000158404605825?through=6000000036984051357
2 CONT Cousin to https://www.geni.com/people/Jacob-Back/6000000007721175195?through=6000000019235927943
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT === Joseph Back
2 CONT *BIRTH: 1745, Orange County, Virginia, USA
2 CONT *DEATH: 11 May 1819 (aged 73–74), Harlan County, Kentucky, USA
2 CONT * BURIAL: Burial Details Unknown, Specifically: Buried near his cabin
2 CONT *Son of Johann Heinrich “John Henry Back” of Culpeper County and Anna Maria (Hoffman) Back 
2 CONT *Husband of Elizabeth (Hoffman-Maggard) Back 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===Family 
2 CONT
2 CONT His father, Johann Heinrich Bach, had changed the spelling of his name after he immigrated to John Henry Back, and so all his children then spelled their last name as Back.
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph married Elizabeth Hoffman-Maggard, around 1773. They had at least four children:
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1. Joseph Jr. (born about 1773) who married a woman whose first name was Sarah.
2 CONT * 2. John (born Nov. 19, 1774) who married Catherine Robertson.
2 CONT * 3. Mary (born Nov. 1, 1777) who had a son Alfred Back, and then died.
2 CONT * 4. Henry (born Feb. 6, 1785) who married Susannah Maggard
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/121232474/joseph-back
2 CONT
2 CONT This Back/Bach family is not related to Harman Back (aka Hermann Bach), from Freudenberg, Germany. A significant amount of documented research proves this. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1994, an incorrect genealogy of this family was published, which claimed that this family descended from Harman Back from Freudenberg, even though there was not one piece of evidence to prove it. That incorrect genealogy was ev
2 CONC en reported to DAR, which is why, on DAR's website, they used to show that the father of a man named Joseph Back was Harman Back. However,  that was a different Joseph Back than this Joseph Back.
2 CONT
2 CONT Here is the actual genealogy of THIS Joseph Back...Joseph's parents were Johann Heinrich Bach and Anna Hoffman. Joseph had two brothers, John and Henry, who married two sisters, Margaret and Elizabeth Hoffman.
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph's parents, and their ancestors, were from Thuringia, Germany. 
2 CONT Joseph's grandfather was Johann Christoph Bach Jr., who was a second cousin to Johann Sebastian Bach. (They shared great grandfathers.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph's parents sailed to America in 1740, and they settled in Culpeper County, Virginia (later became Madison County). They lived on 786 acres, along Crooked Creek (also called Meander Run), about two miles from where it flows i
2 CONC nto the Robinson River, just south of the present-day town of Culpeper. This was where Joseph was born and raised. He was the youngest son of Johann Heinrich Bach and Anna Hoffman. 
2 CONT
2 CONT His father, Johann Heinrich Bach, had changed the spelling of his name after he immigrated to John Henry Back, and so all his children then spelled their last name as Back.
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph married Elizabeth Hoffman-Maggard, around 1773. They had at least four children:
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1. Joseph Jr. (born about 1773) who married a woman whose first name was Sarah.
2 CONT * 2. John (born Nov. 19, 1774) who married Catherine Robertson.
2 CONT * 3. Mary (born Nov. 1, 1777) who had a son Alfred Back, and then died.
2 CONT * 4. Henry (born Feb. 6, 1785) who married Susannah Maggard
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph had always dreamed of living in Kentucky, probably after reading about Daniel Boone's adventures. So, on August 16, 1782, he went to Richmond, Virginia and he bought a Treasury Warrant.  That was the first step needed, in o
2 CONC rder to buy land in Kentucky.  The Treasury Warrant cost about $3,000.  It entitled him to 791 acres (please see attached picture). 
2 CONT He actually turned it over and assigned half of it to his friend Stephen Jett (FAG #182789529). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Then they went to Kentucky to find the exact parcel they wanted. ,They found some land they liked near Hinkston's Fork (just south of the present-day city of Cynthiana).  They recorded that entry on Jan. 23, 1783 (see the attached
2 CONC  picture). 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000186365497850&size=large}}
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT They decided to find a second parcel, just in case the first one had already been selected.  ,Their second parcel was along Slate Creek, which was a branch of the Licking River (about forty miles southwest of the present-day city 
2 CONC of Morehead).  They recorded that entry on Feb. 6, 1783 (see the attached picture). Then they went home to Virginia and waited. Over the next few years, they waited for the surveyors to verify their claims, but unfortunately, othe
2 CONC r people had claimed (stolen) their two land parcels first. 
2 CONT
2 CONT But Joseph never gave up on his dream to live in Kentucky. He decided that, whenever he finally did get to Kentucky, he would simply "squat" on some land, and not try to buy any land, because he was so disgusted by the land-buying
2 CONC  process in Kentucky.  (In fact, Daniel Boone was so disgusted by all the stealing of land that he refused to buy any land in Kentucky either.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Please note that Joseph was not listed in any of the Personal Property Tax Lists in Culpeper County, Virginia, because he was living on his father's farm, and his father was too old to be counted as a tithable. His father owned th
2 CONC e farm and all the assets on the farm. Apparently, the tax man failed to count Joseph as a tithable, either he did not know he lived there, Joseph was gone when the tax man arrived, or the tax man simply made an error (probably wh
2 CONC at happened). But Joseph's two brothers, John and Henry, were counted on all of the tax lists in Culpeper County, as they lived on land that had been inherited by their wives.
2 CONT
2 CONT After Joseph's father died, in 1789, he and his wife and children left Virginia, and they finally migrated to Kentucky. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph took the old Family Bible with him, along with his wife's spinning wheel (which is now in the museum, in Jackson, Kentucky).  
2 CONT
2 CONT Along the way, they stopped in Franklin County, NC for awhile.
2 CONT
2 CONT They were seen living there, in the 1790 Census Report (see the attached picture). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph's friend Stephen Jett and his family had been living in North Carolina for awhile. 
2 CONT
2 CONT They lived just a few miles away, right across the Franklin County line, in Granville County.
2 CONT
2 CONT In early 1791, Joseph and his family continued their trek towards Kentucky. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Stephen Jett (FAG #182789529) and his family accompanied them.
2 CONT
2 CONT They crossed over the mountains into southeastern Kentucky, in April of 1791. 
2 CONT
2 CONT They first stopped along the Cumberland River, where Joseph and Elizabeth witnessed the marriage of James Robeson and Sarah Murfit, on April 23, 1791. 
2 CONT
2 CONT A copy of that old document is still in existence (see the attached picture). 
2 CONT That old document is the earliest record of the Back (Bach) family, in southeastern Kentucky.
2 CONT
2 CONT Shortly after that, Joseph built a flatboat and he and his family went down the Kentucky River. 
2 CONT
2 CONT They stopped at Quicksand Creek, in what is now Breathitt County. 
2 CONT They built a cabin there and they "squatted" on the land--Joseph did not even try to buy the land because he had been cheated out of two prior land purchases in Kentucky, in which he lost a great deal of money. 
2 CONT But this land along Quicksand Creek was not too far from the parcel on Slate Creek that he had fallen in love with, several years before.
2 CONT
2 CONT The land on which Joseph and his family squatted on was extremely remote. 
2 CONT There were no other people living anywhere close to them. 
2 CONT He and his sons built their cabin on some land which later became known as "the Round Bottom," because it was nearly completely encircled by Quicksand Creek.
2 CONT
2 CONT His two oldest children, Joseph Jr. and John, went to southwestern Virginia, in 1794, following the death of his uncle, John William Back (the brother of his father), to help the widow with the farm. 
2 CONT
2 CONT They ended up getting married there, in 1795, to two women who lived near there. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Both Joseph Jr. and John continued to live in Virginia for several years. In fact, John and his wife Catherine lived on John William Back's farm until 1810, when they returned to Kentucky.
2 CONT
2 CONT After Joseph's two oldest children got married, he and his wife became concerned that it would be difficult for their two youngest children, Mary and Henry, to find a spouse, because they lived in such a remote place.  
2 CONT In 1797, their daughter Mary turned 20 years old, and so, they moved down near the Cumberland River, where there was a settlement of people. Joseph built a large cabin along Collier's Creek, which is a tributary to the Poor Fork, 
2 CONC and which flows ino the North Fork of the Cumberland River. 
2 CONT Once again, Joseph "squatted" on that land. 
2 CONT
2 CONT It was said that the cabin that Joseph built had two large chimneys. 
2 CONT (Please see the attached article which describes how the Back's (Bach's)  from Thuringia, Germany settled there. Shown is page 2 from the article,  
2 CONT The Post Offices of Letcher County, Kentucky,  written by Robert M. Rennick, in March of 2002, and published by the Scholarworks of the Morehease State University. You can find the full article online.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph died on May 11, 1819, in Harlan County, Kentucky. It is believed that he was buried near his cabin along Collier's Creek.
2 CONT
2 CONT NOTE:  When Joseph and Elizabeth came to Kentucky in 1791, they brought with them the old Bach Family Bible (actually a Catechism, which is similar to a Bible). 
2 CONT Joseph's father (Johann Heinrich Bach) had actually bought that old book, for him, back in 1762.  
2 CONT
2 CONT At Christmas of 1836, when Joseph's son John and his family had moved back into that old cabin that John had helped Joseph build, in 1791, along Quicksand Creek, John wrote down his name, his wife's name, and all of their children
2 CONC 's names, and their dates of birth, into that old book. 
2 CONT That old book is now at the Breathitt County Library.
2 CONT
2 CONT NOTE: It is very important to know that Joseph's brother Henry also married a woman named Elizabeth Hoffman. 
2 CONT She was born on July 13, 1746, and she was the daughter of John Hoffman and Maria Sabina Folg. 
2 CONT Her name and date of birth were written into her father's Hoffman Family Bible (see FAG memorial #167919529), as well as in her husband's Bach Family Bible (see FAG memorial #206381242), which was first owned by Johann Heinrich Ba
2 CONC ch (the father of her husband Henry). 
2 CONT Because her name and her date of birth were written into both Bibles, that further proves that she married into the Johann Heinrich Bach family, and not the Harman Bach family, as so many people falsely claim.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
