1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Vetern of Revolutionary War.  Private 3rd Regiment Virginia Mililia.          FROM "ABSTRACT OF REVOLUTIONARY WAR PENSION FILES" BY VIRGIL D. WHITE.  W8337, Virginia Line, soldier applied 20 Nov. 1832 Pulaski Co. Ky., age 70,born 
2 CONC 04 April 1769 in Lancaster Pa. , living in Wythe Co. Va. at enlistment and in 1808 moved to Pulaski Co., Ky.: Widow applied 23 June 1841, Pulaski Co., age 72, married 1786 Wyth Co., Va.: soldier died 09 October 1836: soldiers son 
2 CONC Adam was 50 in 1838 and was third child of soldier and states mother's name was Margaret Phillipa(marriage bond signed 28 March 1786 and widow was shown as a Phillipi and was signed in Montgomery Co., Va.      I have two possible 
2 CONC birth dated for Henry:   April 4, 1760 (or) April 12, 1762       
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry is believed to have Received a Revolutionary War Pension from Virginia, Rev. War Vet Pension # W8337.  Wife on pension is listed as Margaret.      
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1808 Henry moved to Pulaski County, Kentucky from Wythe County, Virginia. He obtained a 200 acre square tract of land "on the waters of Fishing Creek".        
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Will of Henry Baugh:     I Henry Baugh of Pulaski County, State of Kentucky do hereby make my last will and testament in manner and for following - that is to say  1st I desire that my wife Peggy Baugh live with my son Henry Baugh
2 CONC  as she is not able to take care of herself.     2nd I give to my son Henry Baugh two cows and two head of sheep and one horse, and if there be any other living property, not herein mentioned which I have hereby given to my son He
2 CONC nry, it is my will for him to still hold it.  Also some household and kitchen furniture too ____ herein to mention.  Also I give to my son Henry Baugh all my pension money which I have or may reveive.  Also that he hold the land w
2 CONC hich he now lives on according to the deed that I have made unto him for the same.     3rd I give unto my son Adam Baugh all the personal property that he has heretofore received of me and the land which he now lives on according 
2 CONC to the deed made unto him by me for the same.     4th I give unto my son John Baugh and my daughter Barby Hines, and my daughter Peggy Young and my son Christopher Baugh and my daughter Christene Hendrick and my daughter Katherine
2 CONC  Cormany all the property that they have heretofore received of me as their proportionable of my estate.     And lastly I do hereby constitute and appoint my son Henry Baugh executor of my last will and testemant hereby revoking a
2 CONC ll other or former wills or testaments.        
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 1836 Buried at Mt. Zion Church Cemetery on Highway 1676 Pulaski Co., Kentucky.           
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry Baugh son of Adam Sr. was the youngest son in his fathers family.  Henry received half of his fathers farm whisch was not unusual in the German spearking family, the youngest son gets the farm.  This of course is opposite of
2 CONC  the English practice.  Henry moved in 1808 tp Pulaski Co. Ky.  There route to Pulaski Co. took them thru Tennessee.  It is safe to say that most of the Pulaski County Baugh's can claim ancestory from Henry (except for those in hi
2 CONC s brother Jacob's family) .  In the period around 180's some of Henry's decendants had moved to Lincoln Co. Ky or parts of Indiana (Owen Co.)
