1 NAME Heinrich August /Heinze/
2 GIVN Heinrich August
2 _MARNM Heinze
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 14 MAY 1828
2 ADDR
3 CITY Andisleben
3 STAE Thuringia
3 CTRY Germany
1 DEAT
2 DATE 17 MAY 1906
2 ADDR
3 CITY Lewiston
3 STAE WI
3 CTRY United States
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Heinrich August Heinze was born May 14, 1828 in Andisleben, a small town about 14km North of Erfurt, Germany. He married Susanne Katharina Scharf on June 21, 1849 in Andisleben. Heinrich August Heinze called Andisl
2 CONC eben his home up until he decided to move with his family to the United States. Together they had 9 children. Unfortunately, 4 of the nine children passed away early in their lives
2 CONT
2 CONT Heinrich, his wife Susanne Katharina and four of their nine children travelled across the Atlantic and stepped off the boat "Bremen" in New York on April 16, 1872. Heinrich must have known where he was headed when he stepped off t
2 CONC he boat in New York, because 10 days later he purchased farmland from John and Lecetta Hildebrandt. (It is interesting to note that Hildebrandt is a name found frequently in the Andisleben church records- something that should be 
2 CONC researched further. This may be more substantial than just a coincidence and might shed some light on why the Heinze family moved to the United States and more specifically, Lewiston.) On April 26, 1872, Heinrich recorded a mortga
2 CONC ge for that original purchase He satisfied that mortgage on December 2, 1882 -a little over ten years later. The land that Heinrich purchased had been owned by the Fox and Wisconsin River Improvement, Co. prior to John L. Hildebra
2 CONC ndt's ownership. An act of Congress granted a large quantity of land to the Wisconsin Territory for the purpose of building a canal between the Fox and Wisconsin rivers. The Heinze farm is near the Wisconsin River.
2 CONT
2 CONT One hundred years later, the herd is comprised of mostly Holstein with only one third Guernseys, partly because a neighbor's Holstein bull once jumped the fence.
2 CONT
2 CONT The farm includes a barn for 42 cows, a 12 station milking parlour, heifer shed and calf hutches. The rest of the Heinze children enjoy returning often to the home farm.
2 CONT
2 CONT Improvements are made and new structures are built on a regular basis at the original Heinze homestead. And so continues the dream that started so many years ago in Germany with Heinrich August Heinze and Susanna Katharina Scharf
2 CONT
2 CONT Obituary: EXPIRED WHILE AT A MEAL Henry Heinz found dead at his home. Aged man had lived alone for a number of years - The neighbors becoming alarmed enter house - other deaths.
2 CONT
2 CONT Some time early yesterday morning or Wednesday night Henry Heinz, an aged resident of the First ward expired at his home on Brady street, his dead body having been discovered by Cornelios Schwanz, a neighbor, last evening about 6:
2 CONC 30 o'clock. Mr. Heinze had been about as usual Wednesday and was busy in his garden, also coming up town to do some shopping, but was not seen after 6 o'clock on that evening. The neighbors, noticing the rear door and several wind
2 CONC ows open with no one moving about, made an investigation which resulted in finding the dead body in a sitting posture at the table and from all indication he had passed away while at a meal as an empty cup which had contained coff
2 CONC ee and eatables were before him. Dr. A. Schloemilch was immediately summonned and a critical examination of the body was made but no marks of violence were found, death having resulted from natural causes, presumably heart disease
2 CONC . Henry Heinz was a native of Saxony, Germany, born May 14, 1828. He came to America with his wife and children in 1871 and settled on a farm in the town of Lewiston where they resided until some years ago when they removed Portag
2 CONC e. Mrs. Heinz died here in 1903. He is survived by five children- Mrs. August Kopplin of Caledonia, Louis Heinz of Lewiston, Leopold Heinz of Pacific, Mrs. Carl Schmidt of Scott and Charles Heinz of Dekorra. The funeral will be he
2 CONC ld at 1:30 from the house and at 2 o'clock from the church.
2 CONT
2 CONT More About HEINRICH AUGUST HEINZE: Burial: Silver Lake Cemetery Cause of Death: Apoplexy Fact 1: April 16, 1872, Arrived in New York on "Bremen" Fact 2: April 26, 1872, Original Land Purchase "Warranty Deed" from John L. Hildebran
2 CONC dt Fact 3: April 26, 1872, Mortgage payable to John L. Hildebrandt recorded Fact 4: December 02, 1882, Mortgage payable to John L. Hildebrandt satisfied Medical Information: From Certificate of Death: "Duration of Death: few minut
2 CONC es" Occupation: Farmer
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes for SUSANNE KATHARINA SCHARF: Obituary: DEATHS Mrs. Henry Heinze. Mrs. Henry Heinze, an old and much respected resident of this vicinity, died suddenly at her home in the First ward yesterday afternoon at 4:30 of apoplexy. M
2 CONC rs. Heinze was a native of Saxony, Germany born 74 years ago; her maiden name was Catherine Scharf. She came to America with her husband in the early 80's and has since made her home in Columbia county: the past eight years she ha
2 CONC s resided in Portage with her husband. She was a devout Christian woman and a consistant [et. al.] member of the Lutheran church. Last February Mr. and Mrs. Heinze observed their golden wedding anniversary. Besides her husband she
2 CONC  leaves five children; they are Mrs. Aug. Kopplin of Caledonia; Louis W. Heinze of Dekorra. She leaves one sister, Mrs. Ernest Heinze of Lewiston, and a brother, Albert Scharf of Summit S. D. The funeral will be held from the resi
2 CONC dence Tuesday morning at 11 o'clock, Rev. Haeuser officiating.
2 CONT
2 CONT More About SUSANNE KATHARINA SCHARF: Cause of Death: Apoplexy Medical Information: From Certificate of Death: "Duration of Disease: few hours" Occupation: Housewife
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage Notes for HEINRICH HEINZE and SUSANNE SCHARF: Heinrich and Katharina travelled with Paulina, Leopold, Louise and Charles to America two years after Louis. They travelled across the Atlantic on a ship called the "Bremen" t
2 CONC o arrive in New York on April 16, 1872. The Bremen was an Iron ship displacing 2,650 tons, 318 x 40 british feet in dimension. It had one funnel and three masts. It's top speed was 10 1/2 knots / hour. It made the trip across the 
2 CONC Atlantic in about 12 1/2 days. The Bremen had 60 first class, 110 second class and 400 steerage berths.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of HEINRICH HEINZE and SUSANNE SCHARF are: 4. i. ERNESTINE PAULINE3 HEINZE, b. August 01, 1849, Andisleben, Sachsen, Pruesen, Germany. 5. ii. LOUIS AUGUSTE WILHELM HEINZE, b. January 12, 1852, Andisleben, Sachsen, Pruesen
2 CONC , Germany; d. June 18, 1912, Lewiston, Columbia, Wisconsin USA.
2 CONT
2 CONT iii. KARL KNUTSON HEINZE, b. March 11, 1855, Andisleben, Sachsen, Pruesen, Germany; d. August 02, 1861, Andisleben, Sachsen, Pruesen, Germany. 6. iv. FRIEDRICK ALBERT LEOPOLD HEINZE, b. July 01, 1856, Andisleben, Sachsen, Pruesen,
2 CONC  Germany; d. September 26, 1935, Lewiston, Columbia, Wisconsin USA.
2 CONT
2 CONT v. LOUISA FUSILIA HEINZE, b. January 06, 1859, Andisleben, Sachsen, Pruesen, Germany; d. February 07, 1860, Andisleben, Sachsen, Pruesen, Germany. 7. vi. LOUISE OTTILIE HEINZE, b. March 30, 1861, Andisleben, Sachsen, Germany; d. J
2 CONC uly 03, 1942, Wisconsin.
2 CONT
