1 BIRT
2 DATE 1755
2 PLAC Augusta County, Virginia, USA
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 1826
2 PLAC Harlan County, Kentucky, USA
2 ADDR
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === Elizabeth Hoffman
2 CONT *AKA. Elizabeth Hoffman-Maggard Back
2 CONT * Birth:	1755 - Augusta County, Virginia Colony
2 CONT * Death:	1826 - Harlan County, Kentucky, USA
2 CONT * Parents:	Nicholaus Hoffman, Barbara Elisabeth Hoffman (born Koestnerin)
2 CONT * Adoptive Parents: Samuel Maggard https://www.geni.com/people/Samuel-Maggard/6000000008648598300 and Catherine https://www.geni.com/people/Catherine-Maggard/6000000008649156895
2 CONT * Sister:	Mary Hoffman
2 CONT * Husband:	Joseph Back
2 CONT * Children:	Joseph Back, John Back, Mary Back, Henry Back
2 CONT ----
2 CONT '''CAUTION:''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Not the Elizabeth born 13 Jul 1746 to Johan Hoffman and Maria Sabina Volk.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''This Elizabeth married Joseph Back and the other married Henry Back.''' 
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/168582980/elizabeth-back
2 CONT
2 CONT Her parents were Nicholaus Hoffman and Barbara Elisabeth Koestnerin. 
2 CONT
2 CONT She was born in what was then Augusta County, Virginia. It is now Rockingham County, Virginia. Both of her parents died in 1770, and she was apprenticed to Samuel Maggard in 1771. That was how her last name became Hoffman-Maggard.
2 CONC  Samuel was the son of Hans Maggard, who was the immigrant in his family. Samuel had a brother named David.
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel married a woman whose first name was Catherine. 
2 CONT They adopted Elizabeth.  
2 CONT Samuel's brother David married Margaret Mauck--they adopted Elizabeth's sister Mary.
2 CONT
2 CONT This has been documented in many records. The book covering Samuel Maggard is "Coming Down Cumberland," by V.N. "Bud" Phillips. The book covering David Maggard is, "Hans Maggard's Other Son: A History and Genealogy of the David Ma
2 CONC ggard Family," by Mary Kay Hushman Lavezzari.
2 CONT
2 CONT A copy of the document for this apprenticeship is posted on this page.
2 CONT
2 CONT She married Joseph Back around 1773.
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph Back and Elizabeth Hoffman-Maggard had at least four children:
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1. Joseph Jr. (born about 1773) who married a woman whose last name may have been Hoffman, but her first name is not known. Joseph Jr. was murdered in 1802, supposedly by members of the Ford family (according to Dr. Wilgus Bach)
2 CONC .
2 CONT * 2. John (born Nov. 19, 1774) who married Catherine Robertson.
2 CONT * 3. Mary (born Nov. 1, 1777) who gave birth to a son (out of wedlock), who she named Alfred Back, but then she died, a few weeks later.
2 CONT * 4. Henry (born Feb. 6, 1785) who married Susannah Maggard
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The family migrated to southeastern Kentucky in 1791, and they established the Bach (Back) family there.
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth Hoffman-Maggard Back died in 1826. 
2 CONT She was the first person to be buried here, in what later  became known as The Maggard Cemetery. 
2 CONT It was named The Maggard Cemetery, because of her close connection to the Maggard family. 
2 CONT (Her son John was best friends with Samuel Maggard, who was the grandson of Samuel and Catherine Maggard, who had adopted her.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Her original marker was just a simple stone with her name on it. 
2 CONT In 1922, Dr. Wilgus Bach discovered her grave, and, out of respect, he had a new marker made for her that was much larger, and had information about her inscribed on it. 
2 CONT Unfortunately, he made two errors in the inscription. 
2 CONT First, he had her date of birth as July 13, 1746, because that was the date of birth that he had seen in the old Bach Family Bible for a woman named Elizabeth Back. 
2 CONT He thought that was THIS woman, Elizabeth Hoffman-Maggard Back, who was the wife of Joseph Back. 
2 CONT But that date of birth was actually the date of birth for another woman named Elizabeth Back, which was her sister-in-law, Elizabeth Hoffman Back, the wife of Henry Back (Joseph Back's brother). 
2 CONT
2 CONT The second error that Dr. Bach made was that he had her place of birth as Thuringia, Germany, because he had seen a handwritten statement in the old Bach Family Bible that the family was from Thuringia. 
2 CONT However, that statement was referring to Joseph Back's parents, who were from Thuringia, and who had immigrated to America in 1740 and settled in Culpeper County, Virginia. 
2 CONT Joseph Back, as well as his wife, Elizabeth Hoffman-Maggard Back, were both born in Virginia.
2 CONT
2 CONT The gravestone that Dr. Bach had created for her, stood in this cemetery for 66 years. 
2 CONT In the fall of 1988, some members of "The Back-Bach Genealogical Society" pulled it out of the ground and threw it over the hill. 
2 CONT They then erected another gravestone that was meant to describe Elizabeth Hoffman Back, the wife of Henry Back. 
2 CONT They did that, in order to "prove" the fraudulent genealogy that they would later sell, in 1994. 
2 CONT However, that woman never came to Kentucky. 
2 CONT She died in Rockingham County, Virginia, where she had moved, after her husband Henry had died, in 1808. She had moved there, to live near her widowed sister Margaret, whose husband John Back had died back in 1794. (John Back was 
2 CONC a brother to Henry Back.) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth Hoffman Back (Henry Back's widow) was seen living in Rockingham County, in the 1810 Census Report, with her son Aaron. 
2 CONT They lived right next door to the Hammer family, and Aaron married their daughter, the following year. Aaron and his mother were also listed in numerous tax lists in Rockingham County, up until 1815, when Elizabeth died. Aaron mov
2 CONC ed to Ohio, the following year, with his wife and child.
2 CONT
2 CONT The (illegal) removal of Elizabeth Hoffman-Maggard Back's gravestone from this cemetery was documented in an article written by Custer Back, who was one of the men who removed it, in the October, 1988 edition of the "Filson Club H
2 CONC istory Quarterly." 
2 CONT His article described the fake genealogy of "The Back-Bach Genealogical Society," and he actually bragged about what he and his cousins did, including Troy L. Back. (You can find this article online.)
2 CONT
2 CONT After her gravestone was thrown over the hill, Wardie and Hazel Craft retrieved it, and they took it back to their home in Caney, Kentucky, which they had converted into a museum called, "Memory Hill." 
2 CONT They placed it out behind their house, next to The Memory Hill Cemetery. Shortly after that, some people from "The Back-Bach Genealogical Society," who were furious that the gravestone had been retrieved, and saved, went to Memory
2 CONC  Hill and they had the word "INCORRECT" carved along the bottom of the gravestone. 
2 CONT Please see Find A Grave memorial #207033808.
2 CONT
2 CONT The remains of Elizabeth Hoffman-Maggard Back (wife of Joseph Back) are in The Maggard Cemetery, under that fake gravestone that "The Back-Bach Genealogical Society" had erected in 1988, describing her sister-in-law Elizabeth Hoff
2 CONC man (wife of Henry Back). 
2 CONT
2 CONT However, the remains of Elizabeth Hoffman (wife of Henry Back), and the daughter of John Hoffman and Maria Sabina Folg, are in Rockingham County, Virginia.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT The spinning wheel that Samuel Maggard gave her when her apprenticeship ended, in 1773, is now at the Breathitt County Museum, in Jackson, Kentucky. She and Joseph brought it with them, when they left Virginia.
2 CONT
