1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/121234386/mary-back
2 CONT
2 CONT In March of 1806, Mary had an affair with John Colyer Jr. (FAG #5172556). He was a stranger to the area, and so she did not know that he was already married. 
2 CONT
2 CONT But John suddenly soon disappeared. Mary was frantic. It was said that Mary thought that he was either "lost in the wilderness" or had been "attacked by wild animals." A few weeks later, she realized that she was pregnant. (She ne
2 CONC ver married John Colyer; he was already married; he had married Susannah Graves around 1803, in Rockcastle County, Kentucky.)
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the research done by Dr. Wilgus Bach, in the early 1900s, and published in his manuscript, he wrote the following about Mary and her son Alfred: "...her husband was either lost in the wilderness or killed by wild anim
2 CONC als, they never heard of him again, she had only one child, Alfred Bach, born Dec. 28th 1806, at the spring where the Kentucky river begins." (Dr. Wilgus Bach was obviously being very polite, by describing John Colyer as Mary's "h
2 CONC usband.")
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary's son Alfred Back was born on Dec. 27, 1806. But Mary died, a couple of weeks later, in January of 1807. It was said that she was buried next to her parent's cabin along Collier's Creek, near the Poor Fork of the Cumberland R
2 CONC iver.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Origins
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/121232474/joseph-back
2 CONT
