1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Biography==
2 CONT '''Anna Margaretha Hoffman''' was born on November 27, 1748 in Orange (now Madison) County, Virginia. 
2 CONT Her parents were [https://www.geni.com/profile-64732647 Johannes Hofmann] and [https://www.geni.com/profile-20241125 Maria Sabina Charlotta Barbara Hofmann (Volck)]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT She was known as Margaret and she married [https://www.geni.com/profile-23484047 John Back (Bach), Sr.] sometime before 19 July 1779 in Culpeper County, Virginia. Together they had the following 5 known children:
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34743091987 Susannah Floyd (Back)]; 
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-127904674 Anna Bach]; 
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34798567995 Sarah Back]; 
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34642932102 Frances Floyd (Back)]; 
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/profile-34743091980 Elizabeth Embrey (Bach)]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT She died circa 1831 in Rockingham County, Virginia, USA and was buried in Elk Run Cemetery, Elkton, Rockingham County, Virginia, USA. 
2 CONT ----
2 CONT * http://germannafamily.org/getperson.php?personID=I071737&tree=tree1
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT GEDCOM Source 
2 CONT http://trees.ancestry.com/pt/AMTCitationRedir.aspx?tid=156739478&pid=1467 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT She was the daughter of John Hoffman and Maria Sabina Folg. 
2 CONT Her date of birth was written in her family's Bible which her father kept. 
2 CONT When her father died, he left her 150 acres of land. 
2 CONT (He left land to all of his children.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Around 1775, she married one of her neighbors, John Back. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret and John had five children: 
2 CONT # Elizabeth (born 1777);  
2 CONT # Anna (born 1779);  
2 CONT # Sarah (born 1783);  
2 CONT # Frances (born 1786); and  
2 CONT # Susannah (born 1790). 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth married John Embrey. 
2 CONT Anna and Sarah apparently never married. 
2 CONT Frances married Henry Floyd, but she died in 1815.  
2 CONT Susannah later married Henry Floyd.
2 CONT
2 CONT After her husband John died, in 1794, Margaret continued to live on their farm for a few years. 
2 CONT In 1807, she sold the farm and moved to Rockingham County, Virginia. 
2 CONT It is believed that two of her daughters (Anna and Sarah), who had never married, moved there with her. 
2 CONT Her daughter Elizabeth and her husband John Embrey had moved there, several years before.
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret Back bought 50 acres in Rockingham County, along Wolf Run. 
2 CONT Tax records show that she paid taxes on that property, from 1811 until 1830. 
2 CONT
2 CONT She was also seen living there, in the 1820 and 1830 Census Reports. 
2 CONT Her daughters lived with her.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is believed that Margaret died in 1831, because she was not listed on the tax lists after 1830. 
2 CONT
2 CONT She was probably buried in an unmarked grave in the nearby Elk Run Cemetery.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.geni.com/discussions/233007?msg=1579043
2 CONT
2 CONT There really was a man named Henry Back, who had a brother named John Back, and they really did marry two sisters named Elizabeth and Margaret Hoffman. They were the daughters of John Hoffman and Maria Sabina Folg.
2 CONT
2 CONT But Henry and John's father was John Henry Back (Johann Heinrich Bach). That Back (Bach) family lived about 11 miles southwest of Culpeper, and practically next door to the Hoffman family. The two Back brothers married the two Hof
2 CONC fman sisters, around 1775, probably in a double ceremony. John Back died in 1794, and his widow Margaret moved to Rockingham County, Virginia. Henry Back died in 1809, and his widow Elizabeth moved to Rockingham County to live nex
2 CONC t to her sister. Elizabeth was seen living there, in the 1810 Census Report (with her son Aaron); she was also listed in the 1810 Tax Lists (as "Aaron Back & mother"); and she was listed in the 1815 Tax List as well. Elizabeth Hof
2 CONC fman Back died in Rockingham County, around 1815; her sister Margaret died there, around 1831. Elizabeth Hoffman Back never went to Kentucky, and so she never established the Back (Bach) family there.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
