1 NOTE !   Page 191, THEY CAME TO KENTUCKY.   Henry and Esther bought land in
2 CONT Kentucky in 1811.
2 CONT
2 CONT !From Helen Spurlin, P.O. Box l377, Diamond Springs, CA 95619-1377.
2 CONT ........
2 CONT  DEED BOOK 1850 PULASKI, KENTUCKY (Direct living heirs had to sign. Henry
2 CONT died intestate, 1833, but his land was sold l850) Christy was married in
2 CONT Oct. 1811 in KY
2 CONT      William was still in VA in l8l7
2 CONT      Jacob bought land in KY in l8l2
2 CONT      William K. was born in KY in l8l2.  He was son of Jacob.
2 CONT      Henry Sr. bought land in KY in l8ll
2 CONT  which indicated that several of the family went to Kentucky by
2 CONT "OXCART" (family saying) between Oct 1811 when Jacob and Magdalena had
2 CONT Catherine, born in Wythe Co., Va and Oct 1811 when Christy was married
2 CONT in Kentucky several went together and one was said to have lost his wife on
2 CONT the way.   A guess would be Christy who married immediately in KY, a widow
2 CONT Elizabeth Vaught.   Gasper Vaught was the lst Vaught into
2 CONT Kentucky, bought land in Pulaski Co., l809.  He may have been Henry"s, if
2 CONT Jacob, Gasper and Henry Vaught had land next to each other on Pitman
2 CONT Creek.
2 CONT      A marriage entry in Wythe Co., VA, 23 Aug. 1811, "Henry and Molly
2 CONT Cormany" (A note was added to this information initialed by G.H.W. saying
2 CONT that this is a true statement and that they are their ancestors)  Helen
2 CONT Spurlin believes this entry may have been in error and that it may be the
2 CONT date when Jacob (son of Henry) married Magdalena (Molly )Cormany.
2 CONT      In THEY CAME TO KENTUCKY, by Alberta Carson Kirkwood, it relates
2 CONT marriage document of William and Pheronica Miller (Veronica, Francis or
2 CONT Frances).  This document gives name of Henry's first wife, Catherine
2 CONT Datteringer.  He married Esther Baugh, l795, Wythe Co.,. VA.  She was the
2 CONT daughter of Jacob and Barbara Baugh.  She died in Pulaski Co., KY , 31
2 CONT July.
2 CONT
2 CONT !   FROM HELEN SPURLIN AND MICKEY MARTIN  BOOK, "There Was A Gasper In
2 CONT The Family"   page 9
2 CONT
2 CONT !      HENRY VAUGHT, son of Andrew and Elizabeth Vaught, was born circa
2 CONT 1764.  Henry is on the 1782 tax list, so is at least 16 in 1782 and born
2 CONT before 1766.  He was over 45 in the 1810 Wythe Co., Va census, therefore
2 CONT born before 1765.    Henry married first Catharina Dattering about 1786.
2 CONT The name of his first wife is from a German marriage fraktur for Henry's
2 CONT son William.  The name on the fraktur was Catharina Datteringen.  The 'in'
2 CONT or 'en' ending on German Documents was used to show a female, i.e.
2 CONT Catharina Datteringen, daughter of Mr. Dattering.  The baptismal records
2 CONT of the Zion Lutheran Church at Cripple Creek, Wythe Co., VA, in the
2 CONT original German, give the following spellings for Dutton: Dothner,
2 CONT Derting, Derding, Dollinger, and Dutton.  The introduction in the book of
2 CONT Hoch, Kegly, and Smith on the Kimberling Church Baptismal Records
2 CONT indicates that Doddinger was also used for Dutton.  It is therefore
2 CONT believer that Dattering is another spelling of Dutton. Henry married,
2 CONT second, Esther Baugh, July 28, 1795 in Wythe Co., Va.
2 CONT      Chester Vaught, a great=great-grandson of Henry, listened to stories
2 CONT of the "Vaughts" told to him by his grandfather William K. Vaught.  One
2 CONT story was that Henry, with his family and others, traveled by 2 wheel
2 CONT oxcarts to Kentucky and one of the men lost his wife during the trip.
2 CONT Other than Henry's oldest sons Jacob, and William, no other married Vaught
2 CONT is known to have been with Henry on the trip.  However, Christy Vaught son
2 CONT of Gaspar, is not in the Kentucky 1810 census.  Christy married in October
2 CONT of 1811 in Kentucky.  He may have traveled to Kentucky with his uncle
2 CONT Henry in 1811 instead of with his father in1809.  And he was old enough
2 CONT (29 in 1811) to have had a prior marriage and might have lost his wife on
2 CONT the way.  (This is pure speculation on the part of your compilers.)  The
2 CONT 1810 census Wythe Co, Va does not obviously have Christy enumerated.
2 CONT Henry's group of Vaught immigrants joined Gaspar on Pitman Creek and
2 CONT bought land 1811-1815 next to Gaspar.  Certainly it would appear that
2 CONT Henry is closely related to Gaspar, probably a brother.  Gaspar's last child
2 CONT was born in 1800.  If Henry, born 1764, was a son of Gaspar, then Gaspar
2 CONT and Elizabeth had children over a time span of 36 years.  This is not likely.
2 CONT On the other hand, if Henry is a son of Andrew, then Andrew and Elizabeth
2 CONT had children from 1742 to 1764, a span of 22 years, which is reasonable.
2 CONT      Children of Henry and Catherine were Jacob, William, Elizabeth, and
2 CONT Henry.
2 CONT      Children of Henry and Esther (2nd wife) were Barbara, Isaac, Mary,
2 CONT Solomon, Anna, Sarah, Jemima, Jeremiah and Abraham.
2 CONT      Many of the Vaughts in southwestern Virginia were sympathetic to
2 CONT the Loyalists, and not to the Revolution.  On April 18, 1779, in Wythe
2 CONT County, Michael Henninger claimed in court that a group of 4,000 men
2 CONT under John Griffith had shown Loyalist sympathies.   This group included
2 CONT John and David Vaught and "the old Vaught who owned the mill".  At the trial
2 CONT of these men, John and David were released on their good behavior. Andrew
2 CONT Vaught of Cripple Creek was included in the group with Loyalist sympathies
2 CONT by the testimony of Peter Kinder of August 17, 1780.  (This would have
2 CONT been Andrew Vaught, Jr.)  These Germans took their Oath to King George
2 CONT seriously.  They nearly all named a son "George".  John and David Vaught
2 CONT were later fined 1000 pounds each.  John, David and Andrew Vaught went
2 CONT to Tennessee by 1792.  It is possible that John and David
2 CONT left the county and state rather than pay such a heavy fine.
2 CONT      Two of Andrew's sons went to Pulaski Co., Kentucky, Gaspar in 1809
2 CONT and Henry in 1811.  Two sons remained in Wythe Co, VA and died there,
2 CONT Christley in 1830 and George in 1835.  Of the 7 sons of Andrew listed
2 CONT above, three left wills; George, Christley, and John.  This helps
2 CONT determine their families.  Henry's family can be put together from
2 CONT several sources, but primarily from the sale of his land circo 1850 in
2 CONT Pulaski Co., Kentucky.  That left, by default, only Gaspar, son of Andrew,
2 CONT as the available parent of any open-ended Vaughts in the southwest part of
2 CONT Virginia during the time period 1792-1809.
2 CONT
2 CONT !    From pg. 15 in the "Henry" book.......Henry was b 1759-1765 in Augusta
2 CONT Co., VA.  He was over 45 in the 1810 Wythe Co., Va census, therefore b
2 CONT before 1765.  Henry married first Catherine Dutton (Dottering) about
2 CONT 1786.  Catherine may have been the d/o Phillip Garrad Dutton and his
2 CONT wife Barbara Grinsler.  Catherine was probably the sister of the Adam
2 CONT and Phillip Dutton who are on a land deed with Gaspar Vaught ca 1780.
2 CONT Henry  married, second, Esther Baugh 28 Jul 1795 in Wythe Co., VA. Esther
2 CONT was the d/o Jacob and Barbara Baugh.  Esther was b ca 1773 in VA and d
2 CONT 31 Jan 1861 in Pulaski Co., KY.  Henry d ca 1833 and was buried at the
2 CONT Henry Vaught Cem., Pulaski Co., Ky.   This cemetery was on Henry's land
2 CONT and just off the Stilesville Road.  It has been plowed under by a developer.
2 CONT No trace remains.  The 1840 census with Esther as head-of-household has
2 CONT an elderly man listed.  This man is not the right age to be Henry.  It may be
2 CONT Esther's father.
