1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT 1940 Joseph rented a home for $12 a month.  His highest education was 8th grade.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1930 Joseph rented a house at 1009 Hancock Street, Piqua, Ohio  for &amp;amp;18 a month. His occupation was Sawyer and he did own a radio.
2 CONT
2 CONT US Army Enlistment Record
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph Baugh enlisted in the United States Army on July 13, 1903 at Stanford, Kentucky.  Eyes Blue, Hair Sandy, Complexion Ruddy, Height 5feet 8 1/2 inches.  Served in 12th Infantry Regiment.  Discharged March 28 1905 for disabili
2 CONC ty at Prisidio California.
2 CONT
2 CONT Moved his family to Piqua Ohio in 1924.
2 CONT Newspaper Article
2 CONT Joseph F. Baugh
2 CONT Joseph Farnklin Baugh 88, formerly of 202 Fourth, died Wednesday in Troy at 9:30 am.  He had been ill for several years and had been making his home with his daughters.
2 CONT but had lived in Piqua for the past 45 years.  His wife Ada Ethel dided in 1967.  He was previously employed at Hartzell's and was retired from the Troy Sun Shade Co. in 1947.
2 CONT He is survived by one son, Earl P. of Piqua; and eight daughters, Mrs. Paul Williamson (Bertha), Mrs. James Munk (Lora), Mrs. Elsworth Supinger (Ollie), Piqua; Mrs. Cleda Rittenhouse, Danville Ky.;  Mrs. Ralph McSwain (Bessie), Pa
2 CONC lm Springs, Calif.; Mrs. Robert Alexander (Marie), Dayton; Mrs. Paul Ross ( Gladys), Clermont Fla.; and Mrs. Charles Langford (Dorothy), Troy.  He had 22 grandchildren and 39 great grandchildren.
2 CONT Also surviving him is a sister Mrs. Kinley Bastin of Stanford, Ky.  He was preceded in death by six brothers and sisters.
2 CONT The Rev. Warren G. Smith of the Congregational Christian United Church of Christ will conduct funeral services Saturday at 10:30 am in the Madison Funeral Home.  Burial will be in Forest Hill Cemetery.
2 CONT Friends may call at the funeral home Friday from 2 to 4 and 7 to 9pm.    
2 CONT
2 CONT Recollection of Ollie Baugh
2 CONT Ollie said that when they lived in Kentucky her father (Joseph Baugh) did some farming and he also worked in a saw mill for $1 a day six days a week.  In 1925 when the great depression hit the Baugh family moved to Ohio by train a
2 CONC nd Ollie started school at age 8 in the first grade.
