1 NOTE 2 JUN 1880 Census Jackson Township, Enumeration District 278, Gentry
2 CONT County, Missouri living adjaent to his brother Martin V.  Miller and sveral
2 CONT families away from his brother Ephriam Miller.
2 CONT 17 JAN 1920 US Census Jackson Township, Enumeration District 91,
2 CONT Gentry County, Missouri with single son, Loren living with them and working
2 CONT the farm and living near sons John & Roy.
2 CONT  James left home with his half brother, Jacob Miller, at the age of 21.
2 CONT He had a team of horses and a new wagon and started west from West
2 CONT  Virginia and never was heard from again. (Notes from Loren Holeman)
2 CONT The 3 brothers, Epriam, James Monroe and Martin V. Miller, are found with
2 CONT their families in the 1880 US Census living near each other.
2 CONT
2 CONT Obituary of James M. Miller Veteran of the Civil War of 1861
2 CONT  James M. Miller, who lived one mile northwest of Island City, died at his
2 CONT  home Saturdau afternoon at 1:50PM.  His death was due to tuberculosis
2 CONT  from which he had been a sufferer for three years.  The following
2 CONT  obituary has been handled by this office:
2 CONT  James Monroe Miller, son of Col. John and Sohpia Cowden Miller, was born
2 CONT  in Page County, Virginia, February 19, 1843 and died July 24, 1926, age
2 CONT  83 years 5 months and 5 days.  He was united in marriage to Elizabeth L.
2 CONT  Emerson, June 10, 1869, having traveled down life's pathway together for
2 CONT  more than 57 years.  To this union 13 children were born: James, Minnie,
2 CONT  Harry died in infancy, Jesse M., Charles A. of Montana, Henry, Loren,
2 CONT Harrison, Mrs Alice Workman, Mrs. Dollie Evans, Mrs. Clark Holeman, Mr.
2 CONT Lucy Plymale of this vicinity, with his wife are left to mourn his
2 CONT departure;  also one sister Mrs. Mary Joy of Oklahoma.  There were 25
2 CONT grandchildren and five great grandchildren.  During the Civil War he
2 CONT  volMissouri Regiment, Company G, and served 18 months.  He was engaged
2 CONT in several battles, including Glasgow and Independence.  He was wounded in
2 CONT the service and was soon as he was able, rejoined the service until the
2 CONT  close of the war.  He professed a hope in Christ some 35 years ago and
2 CONT  united with the LaFayette Baptist Church, which hope was an anchor to the
2 CONT  soul both sure and steadfast.  Uncle Jim was a kind neighbor, beloved by
2 CONT all and his friends wre many.  He resided on the same farm since coming
2 CONT to Missouri in 1852 with the exception of 2 years he resided in Stanberry
2 CONT but the lingering for the old farm caused him to return.  In the early
2 CONT days, no careworn traveler was ever turned from his door hungry.  He will
2 CONT  be missed by his loving wife, who constantly kept vigilance by the
2 CONT  bedside for a year.  He had been failing for a year but had been a
2 CONT patient sufferer.  At the last visions of The Beyond flitted before him,
2 CONT  and he expressed a desire "I want to go home".  Brother Jess Guter, who
2 CONT  grew up to manhood by Uncle Jim Miller, but now of Greenbush, Illinois,
2 CONT  preached the funeral.  A message was sent to the minister and he started
2 CONT  at one.  With Mrs Clarence Richardson at the organ, the chior sang "Rock
2 CONT  of Ages" and "Oh how Happy are They Who Obey Their Savior" and "I am
2 CONT  Going Home to Lie No More."  A short service was held at the house before
2 CONT leaving for the LaFayette Baptist Church where the funeral was held.  The
2 CONT Stanberry Masonic Lodge, of which he was an honorable member, had
2 CONT charge of the burial and held a very impressive rite. Intermet was in the
2 CONT  Lafayette Cemetery.  The funeral was conducted by the Pennington
2 CONT Undertaking Company.  The pall bearers were grandsons of the deceased,
2 CONT  Homer & Bob Evans, Wilbur & Ora Workman, Jimmie Miller and Loren
2 CONT Holeman.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Golden Wedding Anniversary of James & Elizabeth Miller
2 CONT Tuesday, June 10th, more than 125 friends and relatives gathered at the
2 CONT  farm home of Mr. & Mrs. James Miller to celebrate their 50th anniversary
2 CONT  of their marriage.  Everyone rought dinners and tables were arranged on
2 CONT  the lawn and everything that a Missouri cook coild think of was spread
2 CONT  before the celebraters.  Mr. & Mrs. Miller need no introduction to most
2 CONT of us.  James Miller and Elizabeth Emerson, daughter of a M.E. Minister,
2 CONT wre united in wedlock June 10, 1869.  To this union 13 children were
2 CONT
2 CONT Line 10799 from GEDCOM File not recognizable or too long:
2 CONT  CONC born, 9 living and all married, but one son and residing within 10 miles
2 CONT of the old home exept one son who lives in the west.  They have 24
2 CONT  grandchildren and 2 adopted grandschildren and 2 adopted great
2 CONT  granchildren.  Mr Miller was born in Virginia and when a child came with
2 CONT  his parents to his present home.  The land was entered by his father,
2 CONT  Colonel John Miller, of the War of 1812, and his sword is still retained
2 CONT  by the Miller Family as a relic.  Mr. Miller has resided on the same farm
2 CONT  for 64 years and is s Civil War Veteran serving two years and six months
2 CONT  in the defense of our flag.  While in service, his mother died and he did
2 CONT not know it until he mustered out and returned home.  Mr. Clarence
2 CONT Richardson made an appropriate talk and Squire Laughlin talked of former
2 CONT  days nd announced the ceremony for Uncle Jim and Aunt Lizzy to start
2 CONT them off properly on their next 50 years.  Mr. Miller has one living sister in
2 CONT  Oklahoma, but she sould not attend.  Mrs. Miller has two sisters and one
2 CONT  brother, Mrs. Romy, sister of Mrs. Miller and Mr. and Mrs. Atkinson and
2 CONT  aunt and uncle of Quitman, Missouri were here to help celebrate.
2 CONT  Following is a poem written for the occassion and read:
2 CONT Uncle Jim's Part.....Oh dear Lizzie I was thinking of the good old days
2 CONT  today.  When we started life together.  In the goof old fashioned way.
2 CONT  Wi our meals together just you and me. Fifty years we've walked together.
2 CONT  Through sunshine and rain. Through happiness and pleasure. Through
2 CONT sorrow and through pain.
2 CONT
2 CONT Aunt Lizzie's Part....Yes, I do remeber, Jimmy of the golden days gone
2 CONT by. When we started out together just you and I. And we've marched along
2 CONT  together through 50 years of life. Through happiness and pleasure.
2 CONT  Through toil and strife. There be roses in our pathway, now and then a
2 CONT  thorn to pierce. But Jimmy, I did take you for better or worse.
2 CONT
2 CONT TOGETHER: when the gates of pearl swing open And we brush the portals air
2 CONT in the sweet by and by, we'll be happy over there.
