2 CONT DEAT PLAC Bays,Breathitt county, Kentucky Hunting Creek Cem etary Johnlived in Mayking, Kentucky on the North Fork o f the Kentucky River theother side of the Pine Mountain fr om Susannah. He was called "CrippleJohn" because of a 
2 CONC ba d limp, the result of his service in the War of1812. Afte r their marriage, John and Sukie lived on a farm adjoinin ghis father's until around 1841 when they moved to the mou th of Pan BowlBranch across the river from Breathitt
2 CONC  Tow n (now Jackson) Kentucky. From1842 until 1845, they were o nly taxed for cattle. Then they moved on toHunting Creek , at the present site of Bays, Breathitt County, Kentucky .John first appeared in the Madison County, Virgini
2 CONC a tax l ists as anindependent titheable on May 25, 1797. He does n ot appear on the RussellCounty, Virginia tax lists for 180 2-1811. They moved to Perry County,Kentucky (now Breathit t County) in 1836. John was noted for being th
2 CONC eowner of th e only wagon in the county. It was of his own manufacture ,being made entirely of wood with no metal, not even a nai l, was used inthe construction. He was a farmer. Buried i n the old Holbrook Cemetery,Bays, Breathit
2 CONC t County, Kentuc ky. MARR PLAC Floyd or Harlan County,Kentucky
