0 @I6000000027912703854@ INDI
1 NAME Miles Wilgus /Back/
2 GIVN Miles Wilgus
2 _MARNM Back
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 19 MAY 1853
2 ADDR
3 STAE Breathitt Co.
3 CTRY Cayman Islands
1 DEAT
2 DATE 12 SEP 1940
2 ADDR
3 CITY Quicksand
3 STAE KY
3 CTRY United States
1 BURI
2 DATE 15 SEP 1940
2 PLAC Miles Back Cemetery
2 ADDR
3 CITY Quicksand
3 STAE Kentucky
3 CTRY United States
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Miles was Clerk of the Quicksand Old Regular Baptist Church from 1891-1898. Miles is buried in the Miles Back cemetery, Quicksand, Breathitt Co. Ky.
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: Miles was a large, rugged man, over six feet tall. He married three times and fathered 23 children over some 45 years.
2 CONT
2 CONT Miles fathered a child, Henry Chester  Back by his sister-in-law, Malissa Strong, wife of Alfred Cope Back. The child was born 3 Nov 1888. Alfred had been dead for 9 years.
2 CONT
2 CONT BTW....Miles was not married to Melissa...
2 CONT
2 CONT It is said that Miles built the first house in Quicksand, Kentucky in 1896. He acquired a lot of land over a period of several years. He sold 15,000 acres to the lumber industry inthe early 1900s. He once owned the land where the 
2 CONC Robinson Experiment Station is now located and is the site of the annual Back Family Reunion. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a bee keeper.
2 CONT
2 CONT Miles is the subject of an article that appeared in "The Kentucky Explorer", Volume 17, Number 4, September 2002, pp.12-14, written by Bob Jones of Raleigh, NC, who identifies himself in the article as Miles' nephew. The article i
2 CONC s entitled, "Miles Back: A Character With Land". Jones begins the article by stating, "Uncle Miles could have been a man with land and character, but actually he as a character with land. He further states that the land formerly o
2 CONC wned by Miles Back was part of an estate that had been handed down from father to son. Further down in the article, Jones wrote,"This man participated in easy living and material wealth without working. He was lucky to have receiv
2 CONC ed an inheritance of land and he then selected several good women to assist him." Jones stated that Miles often said, "A good woman can pay for herself in seven years." In another part of the article, Jones wrote,"Uncle Miles rais
2 CONC ed sheep, but his wife was the shepherd. He produced corn and hay, but his family did the work. He had milk cows,but he couldn't milk a cow. He always found time to sit in the dog trot and shade, or to sit on the plank fence and w
2 CONC atch work in progress. It seemed as though he used most of his extra energy in chewing tobacco and relating past experiences." At the end of the article, Jones stated, "In his final years, he decided that he should gain some knowl
2 CONC edge of religion. Although he never attended a church, he sometimes listened to Caddie on the radio. Before he got ready to die, beyond the age of 85, he was baptized in the North Fork of the Kentucky River at the mouth of Quicksa
2 CONC nd Creek. He had to be carried down from the highway to the river bank in a chair. The Reverend Andy Crase, the most notable Hardshell Baptist preacher in that section, performed the ceremony."
1 FAMC @F6000000013092012877@
