1 OCCU Blacksmith, Farmer & Landowner, Free Mason
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Ike was a 33 degree Freemason, TN Civil War Hero and a leader in his community. He joined the 1st Tennessee Regiment Aug 10, 1861 and served as its blacksmith until captured at Somerset, KY  Aug 1, 1863. He was hel
2 CONC d in Camp Douglas in Chicago until released in March 1865. After the war he moved his family to Carrollton AR where he was the first to start rebuilding after the town that had been destroyed in the war. He built his blacksmith sh
2 CONC op and home across the street from the Carrollton County Courthouse and he homesteaded a farm a short distance out of town. Ike donated a portion of his land to build the areas school house.  He was well known for giving Carrollto
2 CONC n running fresh "drinking water for both man beast at the town square".... he made an auger  to hollow out 4 ft long logs and built a  water pipe from a spring on a cliff across the river from town. The Carrollton County History b
2 CONC ook says it worked well for many years, and the town was known for it.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Isaac was the son of Silas Wann & Sarah Collins. He married Ellen J. Cooper (b.1834,KY), and their marriage was blessed with, at least, five children; Silas M., Julia, Claudous, George, Valentine, & Sylvia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Isaac enlisted in CO. A, 2nd Reg't Tennessee Cavalry (Ashbey's Reg't) on August 10, 1861.   He was captured by Union forces at Somerset Kentucky on August 3, 1963.  He was confined at Prison Camp Douglas near Chicago until his par
2 CONC ole on March 14, 1865 at City Point VA.* Reference: [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/33513744/isaac-wilson-wann Find A Grave Memorial] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Jun 30 2022, 18:11:00 UTC''
