1 NOTE {geni:about_me} married, son of James T. Miller and Charlotte Collins, husband of Jessie Maud Miller d. cert 11
2 CONT
2 CONT [ http://www.sos.mo.gov/images/archives/deathcerts/1935/1935_00000012.PDF Missouri Death Certificate]
2 CONT
2 CONT RAPHAEL M. MILLER, son of James T. and Charlotte (Collins) Miller. was born January 1, 1876, in Putnam County, Missouri. He was married August 1, 1899, to Jessie M. Stephens, daughter of Judge John D. and America Stephens. They ha
2 CONC ve two children: Raphael M., Jr., born March 13, 1902; Ronald S., August 25, 1905.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Miller was born at a place called Milletsburg, now called Graysville. He moved to Adair County when only about three years old, and was reared on a farm five miles southeast of Kirksville. He attended the public schools and to
2 CONC ok a three years' course at the State Normal School. He remained on the farm until twenty years old, then came to Kirksville and worked for C.A. Robinson in the mercantile business for eight years. At the end of that time, in 1902
2 CONC , he was elected Recorder on the Democratic ticket, although the county then had a Republican majority of about 800. He served four years. During this time he assisted in organizing the Kirksville Trust Company. Immediately after 
2 CONC his term of office expired he was elected Secretary of the Trust Company. He has served in this and other official capacities since its organization. Besides the bank, Mr. Miller is interested in various other business enterprises
2 CONC . He owns several big mercantile establishments, has stock in numerous manufactories. He also owns 3,500 acres of land in this and neighboring states.
2 CONT
2 CONT R. M. Miller is a Democrat in politics, and has served his party in various ways. In 1904 he was elected delegate from the First Congressional District to the National Democratic convention, the first time this honor has been give
2 CONC n to this county in thirty years.
2 CONT
2 CONT History of Adair County, 1911 By Eugene Morrow Violette Page 1058
