3 STAE Rose Hill
3 CTRY Holy See (Vatican City State)
1 DEAT
2 DATE 28 NOV 1970
2 ADDR
3 STAE Topeka
3 CTRY KS
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Calvin Wolfe was born in Lee County, VA, in 1899, the oldest of 12 children.  His father, a farmer, moved the family to Jackson County, KS, in approximately 1915.  It is thought they crossed the country by covered wagon.  His gran
2 CONC dfather, Silas Wolfe, was a wagon maker and it's possible they came cross country in one or more of the wagons built by Silas.    Soon after arriving in Kansas he married Gladys Priest in 1922 in Atchison, KS, and began a family o
2 CONC f his own.  They spent most of the early years of their married life in and around Whiting, KS.  The children attended school there and they visited with family and friends. Both Gladys and Calvin had relatives in Whiting and neig
2 CONC hboring towns.    The family lived in different houses they rented.  Calvin did some farming and construction work, but he also did other odd jobs to earn money for his growing family.  He picked apples, chopped wood, etc., to hel
2 CONC p support the family.    He moved to Holton, KS, in 1938.  While living there he helped build the supply depot near Forbes Air Base at Topeka and he helped build Camp Funston which later was called Fort Riley.    He moved the fami
2 CONC ly to Topeka, KS, in 1943.  While in Topeka they were able to build a home of their own.    He worked as a vice president and general superintendent of Emery Construction Company.  They primarily built residential homes.  One of t
2 CONC heir larger projects was a set of apartments located on 21st street in Topeka.  Calvin was always very fond of his Virginia heritage and George Washington's home there.  He suggested the new set of apartments be fashioned and name
2 CONC d after Washington's home, Mt. Vernon.  The apartments are still in use and are named and designed after Calvin's suggestion. Other residential projects included Cullen Village and Capehart, which merged and were later called Mont
2 CONC ara, located near Forbes Air Base south of Topeka.    The Wolfe family attended church services at the First Christian Church and then the Oakland Christian Church.  He was a member there at the time of his death, though his healt
2 CONC h prevented him from attending regularly.    He was also a member of the Modern Woodmen of America and Local Nunber 1445 of the Carpenter's Union.    Personal recollections of Calvin include a kind hearted man that always had time
2 CONC  for his kids and grandkids.  He spoke softly and slowly.  He loved to sit in his chair with family and friends over to visit with him.  He rolled his own cigarettes and loved to spend time in the garden making things grow.
