1 NAME Robert Lee /Alexander/
2 GIVN Robert Lee
2 SURN Alexander
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 PLAC Kettering, Montgomery County, Ohio, USA
2 ADDR
1 BIRT
2 DATE 15 JUL 1920
2 PLAC Versailles, Darke, Ohio, USA
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 17 SEP 1994
2 PLAC Washington Twp, Montgomery County, Ohio, USA
2 ADDR
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT 1930 Robert was age 10 and lived with his mother at 1 Pearl Street, Covington, Ohio.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1940 Robert was age 19 and lived with his mother on Wood Street, Piqua, Ohio.  He had completed High School and was employed at the Paper Box Factory as a stayer.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert was raised alone by his mother after his father died when Robert was only two months old.   For several years after his fathers death Robert, his mother and sisters lived with his grandfather Sproat at 167 North Pearl Stree
2 CONC t, Covington, Ohio.   After about an11 year period the family moved into a house of their own in Covington.  
2 CONT
2 CONT On June 20, 1939 Robert enlisted in the National Guard.  October 15, 1940 he was called to Federal Service for one year and was trained at Camp Shelby, Mississippi.  He embarked from San Fransico, California May 26, 1942 aboard th
2 CONC e S.S. President Collidge and arrived at Suva, Fiji via Levu Island.  April 15, 1943 he embarked from Latoka, Fiji aboard the ship John Penn for Guadal Canal.  From Guadal Canal on July 5, 1943 he left aboard a converted destroyer
2 CONC  for the New Georgia beachhead.  July 6, 1943 was his first day under enemy fire from the Japanese on New Georga.  On September 10, 1943 it was back to Guadal Canal aboard an L.C.I.  then on November 5, 1945 on to Bouganville Isla
2 CONC nd beachhead.  December 12, 1944 from Bouganville it was on to Luzon, Phillipine Islands. 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Major combat campaigns: Battle of New Georga July 1943, Battle of Bouganville November 1943, Battle of the Phillipines July 1945, the wounded at Manila February 5 1945.  At the time he was wounded he was acting platoon leader beca
2 CONC use they had lost their Lieutenant in combat.  April 3, 1945 he arrived at the 105th General Hospital,  Blak, 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Netherlands East Indies.  He arrived back in the U.S. April 5, 1945 and was sent on to Billings General Hospital at Fort Benjamin Harrison, Indiana.  June 14, 1945 he was discharged from the Army.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Army Service was with  Company M, 148th Infantry, 37th Division.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Promotions:  Private First Class   October 15, 1940
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Corporal                   December 10, 1940
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Sergeant                  April 27, 1942
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Staff Sergeant          November 4, 1943
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Tech. Sergeant         February 1, 1944
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT On October 4 ,1946 he married Ethel Marie Gragg (Baugh) in a civil ceremony before a Justice of the Peace in Jefferson Township, Montgomery County, Ohio.  Marie had one daughter Jeannette from a previous marriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert and Marie had two children Larry and Nancy.  In 1946 Robert listed his occupation as machinist and his address as 926 Huffman Avenue, Dayton, Ohio.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert worked for Brown &amp;amp; Brockmeyer in Dayton, Ohio.  When two men, John Gayer and Paul Stone started their own company, and named it Gayston,  they  took  Robert as the companies first employee.  Over the years the compa
2 CONC ny grew and his position grew within the company.  He rose to the position of Vice President.   In later years as his health began to fail he continued to work for the company, on a part time basis,  as a consultant.  He worked fo
2 CONC r Gayston about 40 years and was a leading influence in the companies growth and development.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT As his health continued to fail he was diagnosed with cancer and became a patient with Hospice.  Robert died at home on the morning of September 17, 1994.  At his side were his wife Marie, son Larry and wife Collen, and daughter N
2 CONC ancy.   He slipped away peacefully that morning as a gental rain fell outside.  By noon the sun cleared the sky as if his spirit were released.  Robert was buried at David's Cemetery just a mile north of his home.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a good man, a man of patients, quiet and tolerant of others.  He was a calm, practical person with good common seance.  Physically he was always slender and well built and up until his health failed he was a handsome man al
2 CONC ways neatly kept, but never flashy.  He was never one much for social events but preferred to spend his time at home with his family.  When it came to sporting events he dearly loved basketball.  Many are the times he would be wat
2 CONC ching a game on the TV and at the same time listening to another on the radio.  He liked to work with wood and was a good carpenter.  He enjoyed fishing and for several years played golf but as he aged the sports dwindled away as 
2 CONC did his wood working.   
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT OBITUARY - Dayton Daily News
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander Robert Lee, age 74, of Washington Twp. passed away Saturday at his residence.  Retired Vice President of Gaston Corp.  Survived by his wife of 48 years Ethel Marie, Daughters, Mrs. Jess (Jeannette) Shackleford of Dayton 
2 CONC &amp;amp; Mrs. Nancy Shade of West Carrollton; son Larry &amp;amp; his wife Colleen of Centerville; sister, Mrs. Velma Lang of Piqua and Mrs. Virginia Delaet of Sidney; seven grandchildren, four great-grandchildren.  
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Funeral services will beheld at 10:00 AM Tuesday from the Schlintz and Moore Funeral Home Centerville Chapel, 820 W. Centerville Rd, (Rt 7245) with burial to follow at Davids Cemetery.  Family will receive friends from 6-8 PM Mond
2 CONC ay at the funeral home.  Those wishing may make contributions to Hospice of Dayton.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Attorney: James G. Kordik  , phone 937-223-8171,: Case # 1994EST300787, Closed 09-Jan.-95.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Lee Alexander  -  Military Service During WWII    
2 CONT Dates                  Events 
2 CONT June 6 1939  Enlisted in the Ohio National Guard  
2 CONT 15 October, 1940  Ohio National Guard Federalized  
2 CONT October, 1942  OH National Guard to Camp Shelby, Missisippi for training  
2 CONT January, 1942  Promoted to Corperal  
2 CONT April, 1942  Promoted to Sergeant Company "M" 148th Infantry  
2 CONT May, 1942  Left by ship for Pacific Theater  
2 CONT July, 1942  First letter from home arrived  
2 CONT September, 1942   Second letter arrived  
2 CONT July, 1943  Battle of Munda on New Georgia Island  
2 CONT October 27, 1943  Awarded Combat Infantry Badge  
2 CONT November, 1943  Battle of Bouganville  
2 CONT December, 1943  Promoted to Staff Sergeant  
2 CONT February, 1944  Promoted to Tech Sergeant  
2 CONT January, 1945  Battle of Phillipines  
2 CONT February 5. 1945  Wounded in the battle of Manila  
2 CONT February 5, 1945  Moved to 54th Evacuation Hospital  
2 CONT March 1, 1945  Casualty telegram sent to mother by War Department  
2 CONT March 12, 1945  Moved to 105th Hospital  
2 CONT April 1, 1945  Sent by air ambulance to San Fransico  
2 CONT April4, 1945  Sent to General Hospital, Fort Benjamin Harrison, Indiana  
2 CONT June 15, 1945  Discharged from Arm Service  
2 CONT October 12, 1947  Received WWII Victory Medal &amp; American Defense Medal   
2 CONT by mail at 266 1/2 Obell Ct. Dayton, Ohio  
2 CONT
2 CONT Army Service 4 years and 8 months    
2 CONT Overseas service 35 months    
2 CONT
2 CONT Medals &amp; Ribbons    
2 CONT Combat Infantry Badge, Asiatic Pacific Theater Ribbon with 4 Bronze Stars,    
2 CONT Philippine Liberation Ribbon with one bronze star, Purple Heart,    
2 CONT American Defense Ribbon, Good Conduct Metal    
2 CONT Weapons Badges: Rifle Expert, Carbine Expert, Heavy Machinegun Expert.    
2 CONT
