0 @I6000000206650437834@ INDI
1 NAME Lydia Emarine /Blevins/
2 GIVN Lydia Emarine
2 SURN Blevins
2 _MARNM Baugh
1 SEX F
1 BURI
2 PLAC Mount Zion Methodist Cemetery, Science Hill,                                                                                                Pulaski County,                                                                           
3 CONC                      Kent
2 ADDR
1 BIRT
2 DATE 29 APR 1849
2 PLAC Wayne County, Kentucky, USA
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 18 APR 1920
2 PLAC Pulaski County, Kentucky, USA
2 ADDR
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} The earliest Blevins descendants were South Carolina "Longhunters" who came to Kaintucke, before Kentucky became a state in 1790. An early direct ancestor was Jonathan Blevins Sr. who married Katie Troxell . Their 
2 CONC son Jonathan Blevins Jr. married Lucinda Roberts and were the parents of Lydia Blevins. (See note below). Lydia Blevins was likely named for her maternal grandmother, Lydia Ponder Roberts. These early families included Revolutiona
2 CONC ry war Tory sympathizers and supporters of the Cherokee Nation. Lydia Blevins would later marry Silas Green Baugh, a descendant of a Revolutionary war soldier.
2 CONT
2 CONT Silas and Lydia (Blevins) Baugh raised seven children in an old log in Pulaski County: son Tim married Susan Owens; daughter Ida married Adam Zeller; daughter Ada married Prentice Floyd; son Joe married Allie Finnicum; and daughte
2 CONC r Lucretia (Crecy) married Harry Ruckel. Daughter Mary Ann died at the age of 18. Great granddaughter, Juanita, lived near the old log house where Silas and Lydia lived and raised their family and would later tell of a huge rock o
2 CONC n the property where her grandmother and her siblings played. In late 1905, after 40 years of being a sharecropper on rented property, Silas and Lydia bought an 81 acre farm.
2 CONT
2 CONT Silas and Lydia lived on this farm until Silas died of tuberculosis in Oct 1918. Before his death, documents show that "S.G. (Silas) Baugh & Liddy (Lydia) Baugh, his wife 'sold' land for $1.00 and love and affection, ~81 acres, to
2 CONC  their son Timothy Perkins Baugh". (Recorded in the Pulaski Co KY Deed BK 8-1, Mar 1 1919.)
2 CONT
2 CONT According to grandson Lester Floyd his Grandma Lydia died at the home his parents', Prentice and Ada Floyd in 1920. The Somerset Journal Newspaper reported on Friday April 23, 1920 for Mt. Zion: " 'Aunt' Lydia Baugh, who died at B
2 CONC uncombe, was brought here for burial last Monday".
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: For many years it was believed that the father of Lydia Blevins Baugh was Tim Blevins with no reference to the name Jonathan Blevins Jr. Completing the 1920 Kentucky Death Certificate for Lydia Blevins Baugh, her son in law,
2 CONC  Harry Ruckel named Tim Blevins as Lydia's father. Years of family history research could not identify the lineage of Tim Blevins in the Blevins' genealogy. Then, in 2007, Lydia's great granddaughter Sherry made a significant disc
2 CONC overy from Court Documents found on the Internet regarding the enigmatic Tim Blevins. A 1859, Aug: Kentucky Contested Election document had been filed by a candidate who lost the 4th KY Congressional District by 11 votes. The cand
2 CONC idate was contesting that a voter, Shadrack Blevins, was not eligible to vote. In the Blevins family history, Shadrack Blevins was a known son of Jonathan Blevins Jr. however, testimony recorded in the court document noted "that t
2 CONC he father of Shadrack Blevins was Tim Blevins (so called)". (Source: Stated in court records by Emmanuel Davis, Calvin Bell, J.J. Shepherd _United States congressional serial set_ Vol 2 #3, Vol 4 #11. 1859-60. Washington: US. GPO.
2 CONC  via Google book.
2 CONT After 87 years, the mystery of Jonathan and Tim Blevins had been solved. Tim Blevins and Jonathan Blevins Jr. were one and the same person and the father of Lydia Blevins Baugh.
2 CONT Contributor: barbara (48929225)* Reference: [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/13472969/lydia_emarine_baugh Find A Grave Memorial] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Jun 20 2024, 5:20:30 UTC''
