2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE AUG 1914
2 PLAC Buffalo County, Wisconsin, USA
2 ADDR
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Harvey J. Hakes came to Rock Falls Wi. in 1862 to take charge of the farm of his brother Joseph who had enlisted in the Army.  Upon his brother's return 3 years later, he bought a farm of his own.  He married Nov. 
2 CONC 8 1868 to Miss Mary Ellen Morgan of Trempeleau co. To this union 11 children were born, 4 died in infancy. Lettie died in 1897 at the age of 22. 6 are still living, Mrs. P.D. Kiunear, Earl and Edith of Phoenix, AZ., Mrs. Richard A
2 CONC lbert, Nancy and Lida of Mondovi.  In the year 1888 he moved his family to a farm in the town of Brunswick, Eau Claire county. On Nov. 12 1888 Mrs. Hakes died. In 1895 the family returned to a farm in Pepin county near where he fi
2 CONC rst settled.  They lived there until 1901, when Mr. Hakes bought the little farm near Mondovi, which has been their home since that time. Mr. Hakes united with the Baptist Church and has remained true to the faith during a long an
2 CONC d useful life.  He was a man who was very much devoted to his home and family, always working for their sustenance and comfort. He has been in poor health for the past year, and at times has been very frail.  Still, he had felt so
2 CONC  well and been so active and happy for the past few months, that his family was poorly prepared for the shock Sunday morning when after he had built the kitchen fire as usual, and attended to various outside duties, they found him
2 CONC  laying upon the hay on the barn floor. Evidently his life had gone out as quickly and easily as the snuffing out of a candle.  While no words can express our loneliness, we feel that if he must leave us, the dear Father was merci
2 CONC ful to thus call home his precious ripened spirit on a quiet sabbath morning without pain or even a struggle. 
2 CONT   His Children.* Reference: [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/110928725/harvey-j-hakes Find A Grave Memorial] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Dec 1 2021, 5:35:55 UTC''
