1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Murial Joy (Dixon) Caudill was the daughter of James C. and Evelyn "Eva" (Back) Dixon.  She married Wendell Caudill on February 5, 1921, in Whitesburg, Letcher County, KY.  They had six children: Virginia Bess Caud
2 CONC ill, Jewel Caudill, Wendell James Caudill, Wilma Jean Caudill, John Wendell Caudill and Norman Wesley Caudill.  Three children, Jewel, Wendell, and Wilma, died at birth.
2 CONT
2 CONT - - - - - -
2 CONT
2 CONT The obituary of Murial Joy (Dixon) Caudill as published in the June 17, 1937, issue of the Mountain Eagle (Page 1):
2 CONT
2 CONT Death Takes High-Splint Mother On Wednesday June 9
2 CONT
2 CONT The death angel called at the home of Wendell Caudill of High Splint in Harlan Co. early Wednesday morning June 9th and took his beloved wife.  She was the daughter of the late Jim Dixon formerly of this county, and was loved by a
2 CONC ll who knew her.  She was a teacher in the graded schools of this county before her marriage.  She had been in poor health for some time and remained for a while in the state sanatorium in Norton, Kansas, of which state she was a 
2 CONC resident for about ten years.  She became a member of the Regular Baptist church about a year ago.
2 CONT
2 CONT Burial took place Friday morning at 10 o'clock in the Caudill grave yard near UZ, Ky.  Besides her husband and three children she leaves two sisters of this county, Mrs. William Caudill of UZ, Ky., and Mrs. Curtis Asher of Blackey
2 CONC , Ky.  Also three half sisters and a half brother of Jackson, Kentucky.
2 CONT
2 CONT The family of the deceased wishes to thank all those who showed so much kindness and respect and who gave such lovely flowers especially the church of High Splint in Harlan county for their gift of beautiful flowers.  Our grief ov
2 CONC er the loss of our dear sister is very great and we feel that enough cannot be said, because she was such a noble and gracious woman and such a kind and loving mother. - Mrs. Curtis Asher.
