0 @N6000000002045244027@ NOTE Henry was born three years after his father immigrated to America.  They were living in the Germanna Colony founded by Lt. Gov. Spotswood.
0 @N6000000003505989387@ NOTE [BeverlyCasey-BCrazy.ged]
1 CONT
1 CONT [Phillippe.FTW]
0 @N6000000002045244038@ NOTE Elizabeth's father was one of the original settlers in the Germanna Colony of Lt. Gov. Spotswood of Virginia.
0 @N6000000002045244346@ NOTE John is a direct descendant of Johannes Sebastian Bach, the noted German composer. His linage is as follows:  son of Henry Back, Sr., born 1740 in Orange Co. VA;  son of Hermann Bach, born 13 May 1708 in Freudenberg, Germany; son 
1 CONC of Johannes Bach, III, born 22 Nov 1674 in Freudenberg, Germany; son of Johannes Christian Bach, born 24 May 1638 in Freudenberg, Germany;  son of Johannes Sebastian Bach, born 1612 in Freudenberg, Germany; son of Henrich Bach, bo
1 CONC rn 1575 in Freudenberg, Germany; son of Erich Bach, born 1540 in Freudenberg, Germany. John and Catherine lived in Knox and Harlan Counties in Kentucky before settling finally in what is now Breathitt Co.KY.  They first purchased 
1 CONC land there in 1836.  The land at that time was part of Perry Co. KY (Breathitt County was not formed until 1840).  Recorded in the Perry Co. KY Deed Book D, page 110, is the purchase of approximately 2500 acres of land for $2000 f
1 CONC rom Wiley Cope by John Back and his eldest son, Joseph. His name appears in the Perry Co. Tax Lists until 1840 when his name then listed in the Breathitt Co. Tax Lists until his death in 1853. John Back is mentioned in J.Green Tri
1 CONC mble's book "Recollections of Breathitt" where he states that four of the most prominent families on Quicksand in the 1840s were the Backs, the Hagins, the Copes, and the Kashs.  He further states that John Back was noted for bein
1 CONC g the owner of the only wagon in the county.  He said it was of his own manufacture, being made entirely of wood; no metal, not evan a nail, was used inits construction.  Trimble said that John Back used his wagon to haul corn fro
1 CONC m that part of his farm known as the Round Bottom. The Round Bottom farm owned by John Back, Sr. remained in the Back family until 1940, when Cleveland Back, grandson of John Back, Sr., sold the place and moved to Preble Co. Ohio.
1 CONC  It is known that the only home of John and Catherine on Quicksand stood somewhere below the Roark Cemetery on the Sanford Brown farm.  It has been described as a large weatherboarded log house with a room having built-in bookshel
1 CONC ves on several of its walls.  Also on the farm, a short distance from their house, near the mouth of Roark Branch, stood a grist mill.  John Back operated the mill, which is said to have been the first mill in the Noctor community
1 CONC . John Back was talented in several fields, as were many men of his day. He was a farmer, millwright, stone mason, carpenter, and a cabinet maker.  A beautiful walnut and poplar corner cabiner that he made is still owned by a desc
1 CONC endant in the family.  He manufactured all the tools he needed from wood. Among John and Catherine's possessions was a clock with wooden works. A descendant of John Back wrote the following about him:  "John Back in his declining 
1 CONC years, being a devout man and believing in the word of God, read from his German Bible in that unknown language to many listeners, the same message that we hear today. He was greatly impressed with the great statemens of his day f
1 CONC rom Washington to Zachary Taylor. He could enumerate the great events as they occurred and to this day the examples set before his children are noted to the fifth generation.  He was a follower of the Jacksonian Democracy and a tr
1 CONC ue believer in the Baptist Faith and principles as are many of his descendants.  He believed that to slay by the sword, by the sword you should be slain."
0 @N6000000002045244266@ NOTE Joseph and Permelia are listed on the 1850 Breathitt Co. KY Census. Living next to them are their parents, Stephen Hogg, Sr. and John and Katherine Back.
0 @N6000000002045244292@ NOTE Susannah is listed on the 1880 Breathitt Co. KY Census as a widow.  Her son William G. 35, a single man, is living with her.
0 @N6000000002045244279@ NOTE Lewis and Margaret are listed on the 1850 Breathitt Co. KY Census. Lewis and Zerilda had a child out of wedlock.
0 @N6000000002045244333@ NOTE According to information recorded in the Back-Bach Family History Book, John was born in Russell County, VA.  Before June 1811, his family moved to the Poor Fork of the Cumberland River, where they lived on 175 acres. John's grand
1 CONC mother, Elizabeth Hoffman Back, lived on 100 acres in the same area, near Oven Fork.  His parents moved their family to the area of Quicksand Creek in Perry Co. KY (now Breathitt Co.) in 1836 where they purchased a large tract of 
1 CONC land .  This purchase is recorded in Perry County, KY Deed Book B, page 110.  The record reads as follows:  " On 14 Oct 1836 Wiley Cope Sr., to John Back and Joseph Back, his son, both parties of Perry County for $2000 Tract on Qu
1 CONC icksand CR. at the mouth of School House Branch which was in possession of James Cope, Sr, 2500 acres, more or less, excepting 100 acres already sold to William Strong 6 Nov 1833. The same land conveyed to said Wiley Cope by John 
1 CONC G. South, Atty, in fact for Jno. K. Brown and others by deed on 19 October 1827, also by deed from John McKinley on 20 May 1824.  All of his land on Quicksand Cr. with buildings, Wiley Cope. Wit: Isaac Back, Lewis Back, Stephen Ho
1 CONC gg, Daniel Duff.  Presented 18 Oct 1836.  Recorded 7 Nov 1836. John was first listed as a separate tithe in Perry County in 1833.  From 1840 to 1867, John owned from 100 to 650 acres on Quicksand, and quite a bit of livestock.  He
1 CONC  was described in 1845 by Hiram D. Back as having a dark complexion with black hair and brown eyes. John and Elizabeth are listed on the 1840 -1880 Breathitt Co. Census records.  John died of cancer in 1886 in Noctor, KY. Elizabet
1 CONC h died 13 years later. , , , ,
0 @N6000000002045244318@ NOTE Solomon and Jane moved to Breathitt County, KY by 1840 and settled on Quicksand Creek.  He raised and butchered beef cattle, selling the meat and also tanning the hides for sale. Some of the tanned leather was kept for his brother
1 CONC , Lewis, who made shoes for their families.  Solomon appeared on the tax lists of Breathitt County from 1840 to 1846 with some livestock but no land.  After 1847, he had 50 acres on Meatscaffold Branch of Quicksand, and he added 1
1 CONC 75 acres in 1853.  He owned 300 acres in 1855 and continued to build his holdings until he owned 3000 acres in 1866.  In addition to cattle, he raised corn and wheat.    Solomon's son Hiram D. Back, once described his father as ha
1 CONC ving a light complexion with light hair and blue eyes. Solomon and Jane are listed on the 1850 Breathitt Co. KY Census. They are living next to his brother John and Elizabeth Back. They are also listed on the 1860 and 1870 Breathi
1 CONC tt Co. Census records.
0 @N6000000002045244305@ NOTE Isaac and Rachel are listed on the 1850 Breathitt Co. KY Census with the first five children.
0 @N6000000001573911889@ NOTE Information from "The History of Mansfield, Texas Mid 1800-1965" published 1996.
1 CONT
1 CONT Jacob Back and his wife came to Dallas County, Texas, from Wayne County, Kentucky in late 1850 or early 1851. In December 1853, he applied for preemption of 320 acres about 5 miles east of Mansfield, Tarrant County, thus becoming 
1 CONC one of the original land grantees in Texas. The acreage became known as the old Back Survey and a patent was issued in 1859.
1 CONT
1 CONT Jacob B. Back joined the Confederate Army in 1861 and was assigned to the Mansfield Rifle Guards as 4th Sergeant.  He later joined Darnell's Company of Dallas and attained the rank of Major.  He led his company in several battles 
1 CONC and had a close call when a bullet went through his hat. On returning home to Mansfield, he was known as Major Back and continued farming and carpentry. Hatmaking was also one of his talents.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1868, he received a cash settlement as his share in the estate of his father, Charles in Kentucky.
0 @N6000000008819661478@ NOTE
1 CONT Moved to Lee Co., Va. @ 1850.
0 @N6000000001573936224@ NOTE Died young
0 @N6000000001573936280@ NOTE Jacob Martin Back settled into the north side of central Mansfield in 1904 at 305 Smith Street. This was convenient for Jacob to ship cattle and hogs to Fort Worth. The family remained in this house, with many renovations, until t
1 CONC he mid 1950s.
1 CONT
1 CONT Jake Back was a past master of the Masonic Order and a deacon in the Missionary Baptist Church. He was a partner in the Back-Bradford Hardware Store on Main Street and served as director of the Mansfield Oil Mill when it was in op
1 CONC eration.
0 @N6000000006186786900@ NOTE Enlisted 8/10/1961 in Company A, 1st Tenn Regiment.Captured 8/1/1963 at Somerset KY, and paroled at Camp Douglas,Il.Delivered on the James River,VA between 3/3/1965 and 3/21/1965
0 @N6000000006186806733@ NOTE Vet # 15055.  Joined Co. A, RG-2, Div-TN-C, 1861-1865 W 
