0 @N6000000001573917079@ NOTE John Thomas Hopson was of Welsh ancestry, according to his daughter, Sudie.
1 CONT
1 CONT Removed from Indian Territory, near Chickasha, to Mansfield, Texas. John T. Hopson is said to be 1/8 Osage Indian. His wife found the Indian heritage embarrassing and did not encourage discussion.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to History of Mansfield, Texas, p. 269: John Hopson was born in Ohio and arrived in Texas December 10, 1835, with his family and slaves. Mr. Hopson served in the army from April 8 to July 8 1836, and was a member of Capt
1 CONC ain Jesse Billingsley's company at San Jacinto. (Note: dates are too early for this John T. Hopson)
1 CONT
1 CONT The History of Mansfield, Texas continues:
1 CONT
1 CONT Mr. Hopson married Telitia Back and operated the Hopson Hotel on the northwest corner of Walnut and Broad Streets. He and Telithia had seven children. They are buried in the Estes Cemetery. (Note: they had 11 children)
0 @N6000000001573936670@ NOTE Died in childhood
0 @N6000000001573936687@ NOTE From William F. Dukes:
1 CONT
1 CONT Aunt Sudie's grandfather was surmised to be killed in Texas by Indians. He never came home from the service, and an Indian was seen riding his horse and sitting on his saddle. Aunt Sudie remembered the story only, and could not at
1 CONC  that time remember the name of her grandfather or his wife.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sudie's Grandfather Back and Grandfather Hopson both preempted land in the same community long before the Civil War. The Hopson homestead was what is now Hackler-Wyatt land, and the Back homestead was east of town some three miles
1 CONC  on what is now the Lewis Hamil place. It was there that Mrs. Stewart spent her girlhood.
1 CONT
1 CONT Early experiences recounted by Sudie Hopson Stewart include standing on the porch of their farm home, with her mother and sisters, and seeing the first train moving over the H&TC track through Mansfield from Fort Worth to Waxahach
1 CONC ie.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sudie's family gave a lot of credit to a Negro Mammy whom they felt saved Emma Susan's live. She was bitten on day by a copperhead snake. The Negro woman ran to the roost and killed a chicken and applied it to the wound.
1 CONT
1 CONT Susie was noted for her expert horsemanship. When she was 16, her father moved the family to town so the children could attend school. He bought the old Mansfield Holt from Mr. Gaulden in November of 1890.
1 CONT
1 CONT Though she said she would never marry a red-haired man, on 7 Feb 1892, Emma Susan Hopson and David O. Stewart were married. The wedding was quite a social affair. He owned a livery stable and always chose the prettiest buggy and h
1 CONC ighest-stepping sorrel mare to take her buggy riding. After selling the livery stable, her husband was for many years in the grocery business with Tom Mayfield.
0 @N6000000001573936775@ NOTE remarried and moved to Ohio after Ed's death.
0 @N6000000001573936839@ NOTE died an infant
0 @N6000000001574983740@ NOTE Maggie Hopson Dukes died of acute appendicitis.
1 CONT
1 CONT Family records say she was 1/16 Osage Indian and her grandmother was 1st cousin of Daniel Boone. Her grandmother reportedly received many letters from Daniel Boone, two of which were given to A. J. Dukes II. They were stored in th
1 CONC e vault at Mansfield Bank at Mansfield, Texas.  When Earl Holland, bank president, committed suicide and the bank was found to be short of funds, Dukes looked in the vault box and found the two letters from Daniel Boone missing, a
1 CONC s well as a large amount of money.
1 CONT
1 CONT Maggie won a beauty contest in Mansfield. A vote could be cast by buying $1 worth of groceries.  She won an elegant pin-on watch with fancy gold hands and numerals. It was stolen from her closet by a servant.
0 @N6000000001573936347@ NOTE Graduated from Mansfield Academy, Mansfield, Texas and attended Decatur Baptist College and received a diploma from Brantley Draughon Business College in Fort Worth, Texas.  He first worked as a bookkeeper at Mansfield State Bank 
1 CONC and later at Beck & Bradford Hardware, both in downtown Mansfield.
1 CONT
1 CONT He married Dora Irene Prater on December 26, 1912, and continued to live in Mansfield. In 1919, however, the family moved about two miles from Mansfield, in Johnson County, to try their hand at farming. They were regular attendant
1 CONC s at the Baptist Church. Claud was a deacon and church treasurer for many years, and Irene a member of the women's group.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1940, Claud and his family moved to his father's old home at 305 Smith Street, Mansfield, where he had grown up.
1 CONT
1 CONT Claud Back held the positions of City Secretary, School Board Member, Mayor, and Manager of Farmer's Lumber Company.
1 CONT
1 CONT Irene Prater Back was a member of Mansfield Chapter 876 Order of Eastern Star and was a Past Worthy Matron. After her husband's death in 1951, she lived on Hunt Street in Mansfield in a house built by Forrest Bratton.
0 @N6000000001573936364@ NOTE Coll Back was born east of Mansfield on his father's 156 acre farm. The land had been purchased from A.K. Marrs in 1888 and was part of the George Greer Survey.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1894, the family moved to 5-1/2 acres on the north side of central Mansfield. Coll attended Decatur Baptist College and married Lula Beatrice Deakle in 1912.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1915, the couple and their new child, Martha Launa, moved back to the family farm east of Mansfield and remained there 45 years, increasing the acreage to 257 over the years.
0 @N6000000001573936306@ NOTE Samuel Marrs was the only son of his parents. He attended school at Mansfield, Texas, and his early manhood was devoted to farming and stock raising. He was a leading businessman in Mansfield, at first a hardware merchant, and lat
1 CONC er took a prominent part of the First National Bank, serving as its first president.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ill health compelled him to retire from his position, and his last years were devoted to supervising his investments and farm interests.
